const e=[{id:"sv-2-061",phase:2,topic:"2.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"A billing-dispute team finds the manufacturing **6M** headings (Machines, Materials …) awkward for its transactional process. Which set of fishbone categories is commonly used for **service and transactional** processes instead?",options:["SIPOC: Suppliers, Inputs, Process, Outputs and Customers","5S: Sort, Set in order, Shine, Standardise and Sustain","DMAIC: Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve and Control","The 4Ps: Policies, Procedures, People and Plant (place)"],answer:3,hint:"Manufacturing → 6Ms. Service / transactional → 4Ps (Policies, Procedures, People, Plant) or 4Ss (Surroundings, Suppliers, Systems, Skills).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The bones of a fishbone are **prompts** that make the team look for causes in every area. In offices and service operations, the 4Ps (Policies, Procedures, People, Plant or place) or the 4Ss (Surroundings, Suppliers, Systems, Skills) fit better than the factory-based 6Ms."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"SIPOC, 5S and DMAIC are all real Lean Six Sigma lists, but none of them is a set of cause categories."}],final:"The 4Ps suit service processes → answer **D**",whyWrong:["SIPOC is a high-level scoping map of the process, not a set of cause categories for the bones of a fishbone.","5S is a workplace-organisation method used in Improve and Control, not a set of cause categories.","DMAIC is the project roadmap; its phases are not categories of potential causes.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 2.1.1; Book pp. 98, 118"},{id:"sv-2-062",phase:2,topic:"2.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"After a fishbone session on *“Late SIM-swap completions”*, the team tags each input **C** (controllable), **P** (procedural) or **N** (noise). Following the usual Measure-phase routing, where should the **procedural and noise** inputs go next?",options:["Nowhere: noise and procedural inputs are deleted because the team cannot change them","Into the X-Y matrix with the controllable inputs, all with the same weight","Straight into Improve, where each one is removed by mistake-proofing","Into the FMEA, where the risk they create can be rated and controlled; the controllable inputs go to the X-Y matrix"],answer:3,hint:"C inputs → X-Y matrix (rank them). P and N inputs → FMEA (rate the risk they create).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book routes the fishbone output in two directions: **controllable** X’s go to the X-Y matrix to be ranked, while **procedural** (SOP-driven) and **noise** X’s go to the FMEA, where the risk of a procedure not being followed, or of a noise input, is rated."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The question asks only about the P and N inputs, so the FMEA is the destination."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Deleting them hides risk, and jumping to Improve skips verification."}],final:"P and N inputs go to the FMEA → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Noise inputs still affect Y and procedures can fail; deleting them hides risk instead of managing it.","The X-Y matrix is used to rank the controllable X’s; procedural and noise inputs are risk-assessed in the FMEA.","Nothing has been verified or risk-rated yet; jumping to solutions skips Measure and Analyze.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 113–114, 119"},{id:"sv-2-063",phase:2,topic:"2.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"One specific enterprise order (*#4471*) failed activation. The team lead wants to follow **one chain of cause and effect** for this single event, asking why each answer happened until a fixable cause appears. Which technique fits BEST?",options:["Fishbone diagram with all six categories","Pareto chart of activation failures","X-Y matrix of the activation inputs","5 Whys"],answer:3,hint:"Breadth (many possible causes) → fishbone. Depth (one causal chain, asking “why?” repeatedly) → 5 Whys.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The 5 Whys asks “why?” about each answer in turn until it reaches a cause the team can act on. It goes **deep** along one chain, while a fishbone goes **wide** across categories."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"One event and one chain to follow: that is depth, so the 5 Whys fits. A fishbone could follow if several chains appear."}],final:"5 Whys drills down one causal chain → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A fishbone widens the search across many categories for a recurring problem; the stem asks to drill down one causal chain for one event.","A Pareto chart ranks counted categories over many events; it cannot drill into one event.","An X-Y matrix ranks many inputs against several outputs; it does not trace a single causal chain.",""],trap:"The fishbone and the 5 Whys work together: the 5 Whys is often used on the most likely bones.",bookRef:"Syllabus 2.1.1; Book p. 118"},{id:"sv-2-064",phase:2,topic:"2.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"A team is about to draw a fishbone. Which statement is the BEST choice for the **head** (the effect) of the diagram?",options:["“We need more experienced technicians on the repair team”","“Customer service is poor”","“Spare parts arrive late at the depot”","“Repeat technician visits rose from 9% to 15% of fibre repairs between January and June”"],answer:3,hint:"Head of the fishbone = the effect: the specific, measurable problem (the project Y), with no cause or solution in it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The head carries the **effect**, normally the Y from the Define problem statement. It must be specific and measurable, and it must not contain a cause or a solution, or the brainstorm will be biased."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Only the repeat-visit statement is a measurable effect with a baseline and a time frame. The others are a solution, a vague complaint and a cause."}],final:"A measurable effect goes in the head → answer **D**",whyWrong:["This is a solution (and a guessed cause) written as a problem; it steers the brainstorm towards one answer.","This is vague and not measurable, so the team cannot tell which causes really affect it.","This is a possible cause, which belongs on a bone (Materials or Suppliers), not in the head.",""],trap:"If an option contains “need”, “lack of” or a fix, it is a solution or a cause, not the effect.",bookRef:"Book p. 98; Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-2-065",phase:2,topic:"2.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"In a fishbone session on *“Slow complaint closure”*, almost every idea goes on the **People** bone (“agents don’t care”, “agents are slow”). The Methods, Systems and Measurement bones are empty. What should the facilitator do?",options:["Accept the result: the ideas show that People is the root cause","Delete the empty bones so the diagram looks cleaner","Run a Pareto chart of the People ideas to find the biggest one","Take each empty category in turn as a prompt (for example “What in our Methods or Systems could slow closure?”) to widen the search"],answer:3,hint:"The bones are prompts that widen thinking; an empty bone is a cue to ask questions there.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Teams tend to blame people first. The value of the categories is that they **force** the team to consider methods, systems, measurement and environment as well."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Use each empty bone as a question. Process and system causes are often found this way, and they are usually more fixable than “attitude”."}],final:"Use the categories to widen the search → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A crowded bone shows where the discussion went, not what drives Y; the empty bones have simply not been explored.","Deleting categories narrows the search further, which is the opposite of what a fishbone is for.","The ideas are opinions, not counted data; a Pareto chart of them proves nothing and ignores the other categories.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 118; Syllabus 2.1.1"},{id:"sv-2-066",phase:2,topic:"2.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A Black Belt wants the team’s fishbone to cover the inputs at **every step** of the porting process, not only the ideas people remember in the meeting. What is the BEST starting point?",options:["The project charter’s business case","The control plan from a previous project","A capability report of porting time","The process map built earlier in Measure, using the inputs recorded at each step as the first ideas"],answer:3,hint:"Process map → fishbone → X-Y matrix: the map’s step inputs feed the fishbone.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s fishbone exercise starts from the project’s high-level **process map**. Each mapped step lists its inputs (X’s), which become the first ideas on the bones."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Starting from the map makes sure that no step is skipped. The team then adds ideas that the map does not show."}],final:"Start the fishbone from the process map → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The business case explains why the project matters; it does not list the inputs at each step.","A control plan from another project describes that process’s controls, not this process’s inputs.","A capability report describes the Y against its specification; it lists no inputs.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 98, 100"},{id:"sv-2-067",phase:2,topic:"2.1.2",qtype:"table",difficulty:3,stem:`A value stream map of a business-broadband order shows:

| Step | Process (value-added) time, min | Wait before the next step, min |
|---|---|---|
| Order capture | 10 | 120 |
| Credit check | 5 | 240 |
| Provisioning | 25 | 600 |
| Activation test | 10 | 0 |

What is the **process cycle efficiency** (value-added time as a share of total lead time)?`,options:["4.95%","5.21%","28.4%","95.05%"],answer:0,hint:"Process cycle efficiency = total value-added time over total lead time (value-added + waiting), × 100.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Value-added times 10, 5, 25, 10 min. Waits 120, 240, 600, 0 min."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$PCE=\\dfrac{\\text{VA time}}{\\text{Lead time}}\\times 100$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\text{VA}=10+5+25+10=50 \\qquad \\text{Lead}=50+960=1010$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$PCE=\\dfrac{50}{1010}\\times 100=4.95\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"About 95% of the order’s life is waiting, mostly before provisioning. The map points the team at queue time, not at working faster."}],final:"PCE = 4.95% → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","5.21% divides the value-added time (50) by the waiting time only (960); lead time must include the value-added time as well (1,010).","28.4% averages the four step-by-step efficiencies; one long wait dominates the order, so the totals must be used.","95.05% is the non-value-added share of the lead time (960 out of 1,010), not the efficiency."],bookRef:"Book p. 105; Syllabus 2.1.2"},{id:"sv-2-068",phase:2,topic:"2.1.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Using the test “does it change the form, fit or function of the service, and would the customer pay for it?”, all of the following fibre-installation activities are non-value-added EXCEPT:",options:["Re-entering the order details into the provisioning system","Waiting for the credit-check approval","Reviewing the work order a second time before dispatch","Splicing the fibre drop cable at the customer’s premises"],answer:3,hint:"Value-added = changes form, fit or function, done right first time, and the customer is willing to pay for it. Hint: words starting with “re-” are usually waste.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s two questions are: does the step change the form, fit or function of the item, and is the customer willing to pay for it? Both must be “yes”."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Splicing physically creates the connection the customer buys. Re-entering, waiting and re-reviewing change nothing the customer values."}],final:"Splicing is the value-added step → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Re-keying is rework (“re-” activities are usually waste); the customer gains nothing from typing the same data twice.","Waiting changes nothing about the service; it is delay, a classic waste.","A second review is inspection or rework; it does not change the service the customer receives.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 117"},{id:"sv-2-069",phase:2,topic:"2.1.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:"A team has drawn the process steps (the **P**) of a SIPOC for prepaid top-up. In the book’s preferred order, how should it complete the rest of the SIPOC?",options:["Outputs → Customers → Inputs → Suppliers","Suppliers → Inputs → Outputs → Customers","Inputs → Suppliers → Outputs → Customers","Customers → Suppliers → Outputs → Inputs"],answer:0,hint:"Work from the customer end back: outputs, the customers who get them (and their requirements), then the inputs needed and who supplies them.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book lists the preferred order: identify the **outputs**, the **customers** who receive them and their requirements, then the **inputs** needed and the **suppliers** of those inputs (and the input requirements)."},{title:"Why this order",body:"Starting from outputs and customers keeps the SIPOC focused on what the customer receives; inputs are listed only if they are needed for those outputs."}],final:"Outputs → Customers → Inputs → Suppliers → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","This simply follows the letters of the acronym; the preferred order starts from what the process delivers and who receives it.","Starting with inputs risks listing everything that goes in before the team knows which outputs and customers matter.","This mixes the two halves; outputs come before customers, and inputs before their suppliers."],bookRef:"Book p. 111"},{id:"sv-2-070",phase:2,topic:"2.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A team maps the complaint-handling process in a meeting room, working only from the written SOP. The Black Belt insists on walking the process and talking to the agents before the map is accepted. Why?",options:["The SOP-based map shows what people think the process is; the team needs what it actually is","The SOP-based map already shows what the process should be, so no further mapping is needed","Walking the process is needed only for manufacturing, not for service work","Agents must sign off the map before it can be used in a Pareto chart"],answer:0,hint:"Three views of a process: what you THINK it is, what it ACTUALLY is, what it SHOULD be.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book describes three views of a process. A map drawn from documents or memory shows what people **think** it is, and almost always understates its size and difficulty."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Walking the process shows the **actual** steps, rework loops and workarounds, which is the map Measure needs."}],final:"Map the process as it actually runs → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The “should be” map comes later, in Improve; mapping from an SOP gives the “think it is” view, not the real one.","Hidden rework and workarounds are just as common in service processes; the book applies process walks to both.","Sign-off is not the reason; the problem is that a desk-based map misses the steps that really happen."],bookRef:"Book p. 100"},{id:"sv-2-071",phase:2,topic:"2.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"A customer requirement for a business-SLA project is being tested with **RUMBA**. What do the five letters stand for?",options:["Reliable, Usable, Maintainable, Budgeted, Approved","Repeatable, Unbiased, Measurable, Bounded, Accurate","Relevant, Urgent, Material, Beneficial, Aligned","Reasonable, Understandable, Measurable, Believable, Achievable"],answer:3,hint:"RUMBA validates a customer or supplier requirement; if any letter fails, the requirement needs negotiation.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book validates customer and supplier requirements with RUMBA: **R**easonable, **U**nderstandable, **M**easurable, **B**elievable and **A**chievable."},{title:"Use",body:"If a requirement fails any of the five, it is not a valid requirement yet and must be negotiated with the customer."}],final:"RUMBA = Reasonable, Understandable, Measurable, Believable, Achievable → answer **D**",whyWrong:["These sound like system qualities but are not the RUMBA test the book gives for validating requirements.","These mix measurement-system ideas (repeatability, bias, accuracy) with the test; RUMBA checks the requirement itself.","These are prioritisation words, not the book’s requirement-validation test.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 112"},{id:"sv-2-072",phase:2,topic:"2.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"The steering committee wants a one-page view of the number-porting process with only its **4 to 8 main steps**, to agree where the project starts and ends. Which map should the team show?",options:["A Level 3 detailed map with every task and decision","A spaghetti diagram of agent movement","A value stream map with inventory and timeline","A Level 1 (high-level) process map"],answer:3,hint:"Level 1 = 4–8 high-level steps (scope). Level 2–3 = more detail, down to tasks and decisions.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s Level 1 map shows the process in a handful of high-level steps, usually starting one step before and ending one step after the problem area."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The committee wants scope and boundaries, not detail, so a Level 1 map is the right view."}],final:"A Level 1 map shows the main steps → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A Level 3 map shows every task, decision and rework loop; it is far too detailed for agreeing scope.","A spaghetti diagram traces physical movement on a layout; it does not show the main process steps.","A value stream map adds times and queues for waste analysis; the committee only needs the main steps and boundaries.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 102–103, 108"},{id:"sv-2-073",phase:2,topic:"2.1.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"An X-Y matrix for a contact centre has four outputs with weights **8, 6, 5 and 4**. The input *“Agent knowledge-base search skill”* is rated **9, 9, 1 and 0** against them (0/1/3/9 scale). What is its X-Y score?",options:["19","32.75","131","437"],answer:2,hint:"X-Y score of an input = sum over the outputs of (output weight × rating).",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Weights $w$: 8, 6, 5, 4. Ratings $r$: 9, 9, 1, 0."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\text{Score}(X)=\\sum_{i} w_i\\times r_i$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\text{Score}=(8\\times 9)+(6\\times 9)+(5\\times 1)+(4\\times 0)$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\text{Score}=72+54+5+0=131$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The score is compared with the other inputs. A 0 rating adds nothing, so a strong link to the two most important outputs carries this input."}],final:"Score = 131 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["19 adds the ratings (9 + 9 + 1 + 0) without multiplying by the output weights.","32.75 averages the four weighted products (131 divided by 4); the score is their sum.","","437 multiplies the total of the weights (23) by the total of the ratings (19) instead of multiplying pair by pair."],bookRef:"Book p. 121"},{id:"sv-2-074",phase:2,topic:"2.1.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"An X-Y matrix gives five inputs these total scores: **X1 = 210, X2 = 165, X3 = 120, X4 = 75, X5 = 30**. The team plots them as a Pareto. What **cumulative percentage** of the total score do the top two inputs account for?",options:["27.5%","35.0%","62.5%","75.8%"],answer:2,hint:"Cumulative % = (sum of the scores of the top inputs) over (sum of all scores) × 100.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Scores 210, 165, 120, 75, 30."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\text{Cum }\\%=\\dfrac{X_1+X_2}{\\sum X}\\times 100$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\text{Cum }\\%=\\dfrac{210+165}{210+165+120+75+30}\\times 100$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\text{Cum }\\%=\\dfrac{375}{600}\\times 100=62.5\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Two of five inputs carry most of the team’s weighted judgement, so they go first into the FMEA and data collection. Remember the scores are opinions, which Analyze must verify."}],final:"Cumulative share = 62.5% → answer **C**",whyWrong:["27.5% is X2’s own share (165 out of 600), not the cumulative share of the top two.","35.0% is X1’s share alone; the cumulative figure must add X2.","","75.8% divides by the total of the top three inputs (495) instead of the total of all five (600)."],bookRef:"Book pp. 121–122"},{id:"sv-2-075",phase:2,topic:"2.1.3",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"When rating how strongly each input affects each output in an X-Y matrix, a 1-to-10 rating scale is recommended over the 0/1/3/9 scale because it gives more precise rankings. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"X-Y relationship ratings use 0 (none), 1 (weak), 3 (moderate), 9 (strong).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book rates relationships as 0, 1, 3 or 9. A wide gap between “moderate” and “strong” spreads the scores out so the vital few stand out."},{title:"Why not 1–10",body:"A 1–10 scale creates uncertainty and argument (is it a 6 or a 7?) and compresses the ranking; it is not more precise, because the ratings are team judgement, not measurement."}],final:"The 0/1/3/9 scale is recommended → answer **False**",whyWrong:["The book recommends 0/1/3/9; 1–10 scales invite debate over small differences and blur the ranking.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 121"},{id:"sv-2-076",phase:2,topic:"2.1.3",qtype:"table",difficulty:3,stem:`A contact-centre X-Y matrix (0/1/3/9 scale) was scored with the **Handle time** weight typed as **3**. The agreed weight is **9**.

| Input (X) | First-call resolution (w = 10) | Handle time (w = 3, should be 9) | CSAT (w = 6) |
|---|---|---|---|
| X1 Call-script quality | 9 | 0 | 3 |
| X2 CRM screen latency | 1 | 9 | 3 |
| X3 Agent tenure | 3 | 3 | 9 |

After the weight is corrected to 9, which input ranks FIRST?`,options:["X3 Agent tenure","X1 Call-script quality","X2 CRM screen latency","All three tie, so the matrix cannot rank them"],answer:0,hint:"Score each X = Σ(weight × rating) with the corrected weights; the largest total ranks first.",steps:[{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\text{Score}(X)=\\sum_{i} w_i\\times r_i$$"},{title:"Score with the corrected weights 10, 9, 6",body:"$$X_1=10\\times 9+9\\times 0+6\\times 3=108$$ $$X_2=10\\times 1+9\\times 9+6\\times 3=109$$ $$X_3=10\\times 3+9\\times 3+6\\times 9=111$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"X3 (111) > X2 (109) > X1 (108). With the typo, X1 led (108 vs 93 vs 55), so one weight changed the order completely."},{title:"Lesson",body:"Weights must be agreed before scoring, and close totals show that the ranking is team judgement that Analyze must verify with data."}],final:"X3 = 111 ranks first → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","X1 ranked first with the typo (108 vs 93 and 55); its score does not change because its handle-time rating is 0, so it is overtaken.","X2 gains the most from the correction (55 → 109), but X3 still scores higher (111).","The corrected scores are 108, 109 and 111; they are close but not tied."],bookRef:"Book pp. 120–122"},{id:"sv-2-077",phase:2,topic:"2.1.4",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A failure mode in a router-provisioning FMEA has **S = 9, O = 4, D = 7**. The team adds a configuration template (occurrence falls to **2**) and an automated pre-dispatch test (detection improves to **3**). Severity is unchanged. What is the **revised** RPN?",options:["14","54","108","126"],answer:1,hint:"RPN = Severity × Occurrence × Detection, recalculated with every revised rating.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Before: S 9, O 4, D 7. After: S 9, O 2, D 3."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$RPN=S\\times O\\times D$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$RPN_{after}=9\\times 2\\times 3$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$RPN_{after}=54 \\qquad (RPN_{before}=9\\times 4\\times 7=252)$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Risk fell from 252 to 54. Severity stays 9 because the effect on the customer is the same if the failure still happens; the actions made it rarer and easier to catch."}],final:"Revised RPN = 54 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["14 adds the revised ratings (9 + 2 + 3) instead of multiplying them.","","108 updates Detection only (9 × 4 × 3) and keeps the old Occurrence of 4.","126 updates Occurrence only (9 × 2 × 7) and keeps the old Detection of 7."],bookRef:"Book pp. 134–135"},{id:"sv-2-078",phase:2,topic:"2.1.4",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:"In which order are the main columns of a process FMEA normally completed?",options:["Process step → failure mode → effect and severity → cause and occurrence → current controls and detection → RPN → recommended actions","Process step → cause and occurrence → failure mode → effect and severity → RPN → current controls and detection → recommended actions","Failure mode → RPN → effect and severity → cause and occurrence → current controls and detection → recommended actions","Process step → failure mode → recommended actions → effect and severity → cause and occurrence → current controls and detection → RPN"],answer:0,hint:"Step → how it can fail → what the effect is (S) → why it happens (O) → how we would catch it (D) → RPN → actions, then re-score.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The FMEA template is filled from left to right: each failure mode belongs to a step, has effects (severity), has causes (occurrence) and has current controls (detection). Only then can RPN = S × O × D be calculated."},{title:"Then",body:"Recommended actions, owners and dates are added for the highest risks, and the ratings are re-scored after the actions to show the new RPN."}],final:"Step → mode → effect/S → cause/O → controls/D → RPN → actions → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Causes are listed for a failure mode, so the failure mode must come first; detection is also needed before RPN can be calculated.","RPN is the product of S, O and D, so it cannot be calculated before those three are rated.","Actions are chosen after the risk is rated and ranked, not before."],bookRef:"Book pp. 126–135"},{id:"sv-2-079",phase:2,topic:"2.1.4",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`A team’s FMEA for a call-routing change lists four failure modes. No severity is 9 or 10.

| Failure mode | S | O | D |
|---|---|---|---|
| FM1 Call routed to the wrong language queue | 6 | 7 | 4 |
| FM2 VIP flag lost during transfer | 8 | 3 | 8 |
| FM3 Hold music fails to play | 5 | 9 | 3 |
| FM4 Callback request not logged | 7 | 4 | 6 |

Which failure mode has the **highest RPN**?`,options:["FM1 Call routed to the wrong language queue","FM3 Hold music fails to play","FM4 Callback request not logged","FM2 VIP flag lost during transfer"],answer:3,hint:"RPN = S × O × D for each row; compare the products, not any single rating.",steps:[{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$RPN=S\\times O\\times D$$"},{title:"Calculate each row",body:"$$FM_1=6\\times 7\\times 4=168 \\qquad FM_2=8\\times 3\\times 8=192$$ $$FM_3=5\\times 9\\times 3=135 \\qquad FM_4=7\\times 4\\times 6=168$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"FM2 is highest (192). Its high detection rating (8 = hard to detect) lifts it above the more frequent failures."}],final:"FM2 has the highest RPN (192) → answer **D**",whyWrong:["FM1 scores 6 × 7 × 4 = 168, tied with FM4 and below FM2.","FM3 has the highest occurrence (9) and the highest S × O (45), but its RPN is only 5 × 9 × 3 = 135.","FM4 scores 7 × 6 × 4 = 168, tied with FM1 and below FM2.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 134"},{id:"sv-2-080",phase:2,topic:"2.1.4",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"All of the following are columns normally completed in a process FMEA EXCEPT:",options:["Potential effects of each failure mode","Current process controls","Recommended actions, owner and target date","The p-value proving that each cause affects the output"],answer:3,hint:"FMEA columns: step, failure mode, effects (S), causes (O), current controls (D), RPN, actions, owner/date, actions taken, revised S/O/D/RPN.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"An FMEA is a **team-based risk assessment** built from experience, process maps and historical defect data. It rates risk; it does not prove cause and effect."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Effects, current controls and recommended actions are all FMEA columns. A p-value comes from a hypothesis test in Analyze, not from the FMEA."}],final:"A p-value is not an FMEA column → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Effects are a core FMEA column; they are rated for severity.","Current controls are a core column; they are rated for detection.","Actions, owners and dates are recorded so the FMEA becomes a living plan.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 126–135"},{id:"sv-2-081",phase:2,topic:"2.1.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"In a billing FMEA, the failure *“customer is double-charged”* has **Severity = 8**. Which action is MOST likely to lower the **severity** rating itself?",options:["Redesigning the process so any duplicate charge is reversed automatically before the customer’s payment is taken","Adding a daily report that flags duplicate charges for review","Training billing staff so duplicate charges happen less often","Asking the team to re-score severity more optimistically"],answer:0,hint:"Severity is about the effect on the customer. It changes only when the design changes the effect; controls lower O or D.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Severity rates how bad the **effect** is. Better controls make a failure rarer (Occurrence) or easier to catch (Detection), but only a design change that softens the effect can lower Severity."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Automatic reversal before payment means the customer is never actually out of pocket, so the effect, and therefore the severity, falls."}],final:"Only a design change to the effect lowers Severity → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A report helps catch the failure, so it lowers Detection; if a double charge still reaches the customer, the effect is the same.","Fewer failures lowers Occurrence, not Severity; the effect of each double charge is unchanged.","Changing the number without changing the effect is not risk reduction; severity reflects the effect on the customer."],bookRef:"Book pp. 129–130, 135"},{id:"sv-2-082",phase:2,topic:"2.1.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"Two sub-teams score the same FMEA. For the identical effect *“customer’s line is down for 4 hours”*, one sub-team rates Severity **9** and the other rates it **5**. What should the Black Belt do FIRST?",options:["Agree written definitions for each point of the severity, occurrence and detection scales before anything is scored","Average the two ratings to 7 and carry on","Use the higher rating, because FMEA should always be cautious","Drop Severity and rank by Occurrence × Detection only"],answer:0,hint:"FMEA ratings are only comparable when everyone uses the same written scale definitions (for example, the AIAG-style severity table adapted to the process).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"RPNs only rank risk correctly when every row is scored on the **same** scale. The book stresses consistent definitions for transactional severity scales."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The 9-versus-5 gap shows the sub-teams are reading the scale differently. Agreeing written definitions first fixes this row and every row that follows."}],final:"Agree the rating-scale definitions first → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Averaging hides the problem; the next effect will be scored inconsistently too, and the RPN ranking will be unreliable.","Choosing the higher number fixes this row but not the cause: the two sub-teams are using different scales.","Severity is essential; dropping it would hide the most damaging failures."],bookRef:"Book pp. 129–130"},{id:"sv-2-083",phase:2,topic:"2.2.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"Time to restore a broadband fault is the sum of two **independent** parts: remote diagnosis (σ = **5 min**) and the technician’s repair (σ = **12 min**). What is the standard deviation of the total restore time?",options:["7 min","13 min","17 min","169 min"],answer:1,hint:"For independent sources, variances add: σ_total = √(σ₁² + σ₂² + …).",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$\\sigma_1=5$ min, $\\sigma_2=12$ min, independent."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\sigma_{total}=\\sqrt{\\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\sigma_{total}=\\sqrt{5^2+12^2}=\\sqrt{25+144}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\sigma_{total}=\\sqrt{169}=13 \\text{ min}$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The repair step dominates the spread: cutting its σ would reduce total variation far more than working on diagnosis."}],final:"σ_total = 13 min → answer **B**",whyWrong:["7 subtracts the standard deviations (12 − 5); independent variances add, they never cancel.","","17 adds the standard deviations (5 + 12); standard deviations do not add, variances do.","169 is the total variance (25 + 144) in min²; the square root was not taken."],bookRef:"Book p. 150"},{id:"sv-2-084",phase:2,topic:"2.2.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"The end-to-end time to activate a new line has a standard deviation of **10 min**. It is made of two **independent** stages. The order-validation stage alone has a standard deviation of **6 min**. What is the standard deviation of the other stage (network configuration)?",options:["64 min","11.66 min","8 min","4 min"],answer:2,hint:"σ²_total = σ₁² + σ₂², so σ₂ = √(σ²_total − σ₁²).",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$\\sigma_{total}=10$ min, $\\sigma_1=6$ min, independent stages."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\sigma_{total}^2=\\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\sigma_2=\\sqrt{\\sigma_{total}^2-\\sigma_1^2}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\sigma_2=\\sqrt{10^2-6^2}=\\sqrt{100-36}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\sigma_2=\\sqrt{64}=8 \\text{ min}$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Network configuration (variance 64) carries almost two-thirds of the total variance (100), so it is the stage to study first."}],final:"σ_configuration = 8 min → answer **C**",whyWrong:["64 is the variance of the configuration stage (100 − 36) in min²; the square root was not taken.","11.66 adds the variances (√(100 + 36)); to remove a known source you subtract its variance from the total.","","4 subtracts the standard deviations (10 − 6); it is the variances that subtract."],bookRef:"Book pp. 150, 176"},{id:"sv-2-085",phase:2,topic:"2.2.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"All of the following are **continuous** measures EXCEPT:",options:["Download speed in Mbps","Number of dropped calls per day at a cell site","Time taken to answer a call, in seconds","Signal strength in dBm"],answer:1,hint:"Continuous = can take any value on a scale (time, speed, weight). Discrete = counts (0, 1, 2 …). Attribute = categories (pass/fail).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Continuous data are **measured** and can take any value in a range. Discrete data are **counted** and take whole numbers only."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Speed, time and signal strength are measured. Dropped calls are counted, so they are discrete."}],final:"A count of dropped calls is discrete → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Speed can take any value within a range (for example 48.37 Mbps), so it is continuous.","","Time can be measured to any precision, so it is continuous.","Signal strength is measured on a scale with any value possible, so it is continuous."],bookRef:"Book pp. 142–143"},{id:"sv-2-086",phase:2,topic:"2.2.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"An operator has exactly **five** regional network operations centres, and the analyst has last month’s P1 incident count for **every one** of them: **4, 7, 7, 9, 13**. These five values are the **whole population** of interest, not a sample. What is the population standard deviation σ?",options:["2.40","2.97","3.32","8.80"],answer:1,hint:"Population σ divides the sum of squared deviations by N; sample s divides by n − 1.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"All $N=5$ values: 4, 7, 7, 9, 13 (a full census)."},{title:"Find the mean",body:"$$\\mu=\\dfrac{4+7+7+9+13}{5}=\\dfrac{40}{5}=8$$"},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\sigma=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sum (x_i-\\mu)^2}{N}}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\sum (x_i-\\mu)^2=16+1+1+1+25=44$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\sigma=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{44}{5}}=\\sqrt{8.8}=2.97$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Because nothing is being estimated beyond these five centres, N is the right divisor. With a sample, s = 3.32 (n − 1) would be correct."}],final:"σ = 2.97 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["2.40 is the mean absolute deviation (average distance from the mean), not the standard deviation.","","3.32 divides by N − 1 = 4; that is the sample formula, used when the data are a sample from a larger population.","8.80 is the population variance σ²; the square root was not taken."],trap:"Exam default is a sample (n − 1). Divide by N only when the stem says the data are the entire population.",bookRef:"Book pp. 141, 151"},{id:"sv-2-087",phase:2,topic:"2.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A Black Belt wants a sample of 300 complaints from three regions. The North handles **60%** of complaints, the Centre **30%** and the South **10%**, and the regions are known to differ. She wants each region represented **in proportion** to its volume. Which sampling method fits BEST?",options:["Simple random sampling of 300 complaints from the combined list","Convenience sampling of the first 300 complaints received on Monday","Systematic sampling of every 10th complaint from the North only","Stratified random sampling: 180 from the North, 90 from the Centre and 30 from the South, chosen at random within each region"],answer:3,hint:"Known subgroups that differ + want each represented in proportion → stratified random sampling.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Stratified sampling splits the population into known groups (strata) and samples randomly **within** each group, in proportion to its size."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"$$300\\times 0.60=180 \\qquad 300\\times 0.30=90 \\qquad 300\\times 0.10=30$$ Each region gets its share, and random selection inside each region avoids bias."}],final:"Stratified random sampling → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Simple random sampling is unbiased but can, by chance, under-represent the small South region; stratifying guarantees each share.","Monday’s complaints may not represent other days or the regions’ normal mix; convenience samples are biased.","Sampling one region ignores the other two; the result cannot describe all regions.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 141, 159"},{id:"sv-2-088",phase:2,topic:"2.2.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which of these data-centre measures is on an **interval** scale (equal steps, but no true zero)?",options:["Server power draw in kW","Priority rank of incidents (P1, P2, P3)","Rack location code (A1, B4 …)","Inlet air temperature in °C"],answer:3,hint:"Nominal = labels. Ordinal = ordered. Interval = equal steps, no true zero (°C). Ratio = equal steps with a true zero (kW, seconds).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"On an interval scale differences are meaningful but ratios are not: 20 °C is not “twice as hot” as 10 °C, because 0 °C is not “no temperature”."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Power has a true zero (ratio), priority is ordered (ordinal) and location codes are names (nominal)."}],final:"°C is an interval scale → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Power has a true zero (0 kW means no power), so it is on a ratio scale.","Priority ranks have an order but unequal, unknown gaps: an ordinal scale.","Location codes are labels with no order: a nominal scale.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 144–146"},{id:"sv-2-089",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"The number of reopened tickets on eight consecutive days was **14, 9, 22, 11, 9, 17, 13, 25**. What is the mean number of reopened tickets per day?",options:["9.0","13.5","15.0","17.0"],answer:2,hint:"Mean = sum of the values over the number of values.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$n=8$ values: 14, 9, 22, 11, 9, 17, 13, 25."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\bar{x}=\\dfrac{\\sum x_i}{n}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\bar{x}=\\dfrac{14+9+22+11+9+17+13+25}{8}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\bar{x}=\\dfrac{120}{8}=15.0$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The mean (15.0) is above the median (13.5) because the two high days (22 and 25) pull it up: a hint of right skew."}],final:"Mean = 15.0 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["9 is the mode (the value that appears most often), not the mean.","13.5 is the median (the average of the 4th and 5th sorted values, 13 and 14).","","17 is the mid-range (the average of the minimum 9 and maximum 25), not the mean."],bookRef:"Book pp. 147–148"},{id:"sv-2-090",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"Five randomly sampled e-mail tickets took **5, 8, 12, 6 and 9 hours** to resolve. What is the **sample variance**?",options:["30.0 h²","7.50 h²","6.00 h²","2.74 h²"],answer:1,hint:"Sample variance s² = Σ(x − x̄)² over (n − 1). Standard deviation is its square root.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$n=5$: 5, 8, 12, 6, 9."},{title:"Find the mean",body:"$$\\bar{x}=\\dfrac{5+8+12+6+9}{5}=\\dfrac{40}{5}=8$$"},{title:"Squared deviations",body:"$$(-3)^2+0^2+4^2+(-2)^2+1^2=9+0+16+4+1=30$$"},{title:"Pick the formula and calculate",body:"$$s^2=\\dfrac{\\sum (x_i-\\bar{x})^2}{n-1}=\\dfrac{30}{4}=7.50 \\text{ h}^2$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Variance is in squared units (h²). Its square root, s = 2.74 h, is the spread in hours."}],final:"s² = 7.50 h² → answer **B**",whyWrong:["30.0 is the sum of squared deviations; it has not been divided by n − 1.","","6.00 divides by n = 5 instead of n − 1 = 4; that is the population formula.","2.74 is the sample standard deviation (the square root of the variance), not the variance."],bookRef:"Book pp. 150–151"},{id:"sv-2-091",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"On one day the **day shift** handled **40** calls with a mean handle time of **6 min**, and the **night shift** handled **60** calls with a mean of **9 min**. What is the mean handle time of all 100 calls?",options:["7.2 min","7.5 min","7.8 min","15.0 min"],answer:2,hint:"Combined mean = Σ(nᵢ × x̄ᵢ) over Σnᵢ: weight each group mean by its size.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$n_1=40,\\ \\bar{x}_1=6$; $n_2=60,\\ \\bar{x}_2=9$."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\bar{x}=\\dfrac{n_1\\bar{x}_1+n_2\\bar{x}_2}{n_1+n_2}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\bar{x}=\\dfrac{40\\times 6+60\\times 9}{40+60}=\\dfrac{240+540}{100}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\bar{x}=\\dfrac{780}{100}=7.8 \\text{ min}$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The combined mean sits nearer the night shift’s 9 min because the night shift handled more calls."}],final:"Combined mean = 7.8 min → answer **C**",whyWrong:["7.2 swaps the weights (60 calls at 6 min, 40 at 9 min).","7.5 averages the two shift means without weighting by the number of calls.","","15.0 adds the two means instead of averaging them."],bookRef:"Book p. 148"},{id:"sv-2-092",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"Process A (chat handling) has mean **32 min** and standard deviation **4.8 min**. Process B (field installation) has mean **90 min** and standard deviation **9 min**. Which process has the greater **relative** variation?",options:["Process A: CV = 15.0% against 10.0% for B","Process B, because its standard deviation (9 min) is larger","Process B: 1,000% against 667% for A","Neither: CV cannot compare processes with different means"],answer:0,hint:"CV = s over x̄, × 100%: spread relative to the mean, with no units.",steps:[{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$CV=\\dfrac{s}{\\bar{x}}\\times 100\\%$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$CV_A=\\dfrac{4.8}{32}\\times 100=15.0\\% \\qquad CV_B=\\dfrac{9}{90}\\times 100=10.0\\%$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"A has the smaller SD in minutes but the larger spread relative to its mean."}],final:"CV_A = 15.0% > CV_B = 10.0% → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Comparing raw SDs ignores the very different means; relative variation needs the CV.","These divide the mean by the SD (90 over 9, 32 over 4.8), which inverts the CV formula.","Comparing processes with different means or units is exactly what the CV is for, because it has no units."],bookRef:"Book pp. 147, 150"},{id:"sv-2-093",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A random sample of **16** field visits has a sample standard deviation of **12 min**. What is the **standard error of the mean**?",options:["0.75 min","3.00 min","9.00 min","48.0 min"],answer:1,hint:"Standard error of the mean = s over √n.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$s=12$ min, $n=16$."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$SE_{\\bar{x}}=\\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$SE_{\\bar{x}}=\\dfrac{12}{\\sqrt{16}}=\\dfrac{12}{4}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$SE_{\\bar{x}}=3.00 \\text{ min}$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"SE describes how precisely the sample mean estimates μ, not the spread of individual visits (that is still s = 12 min). Quadrupling n halves the SE."}],final:"SE = 3.00 min → answer **B**",whyWrong:["0.75 divides s by n (12 over 16) instead of by √n.","","9.00 divides the variance by n (144 over 16) and forgets the square root.","48.0 multiplies s by √n instead of dividing."],bookRef:"Book p. 148; Book pp. 278–279"},{id:"sv-2-094",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Minitab output for 64 number-porting times:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: PortTime (h)

Variable   N  N*   Mean  SE Mean  StDev  Minimum     Q1  Median     Q3  Maximum
PortTime  64   0  26.41     1.92  15.36     6.10  15.85   22.40  33.10    88.70
\`\`\`

The manager wants a measure of **spread** that is **not distorted** by the one extreme port of 88.7 h. Which value should be reported?`,options:["Range = 82.60 h","IQR = 17.25 h","StDev = 15.36 h","SE Mean = 1.92 h"],answer:1,hint:"Resistant spread = IQR = Q3 − Q1 (middle 50% of the data). Range and SD are sensitive to extremes.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Q1 = 15.85 h and Q3 = 33.10 h in the output."},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$IQR=Q_3-Q_1=33.10-15.85=17.25 \\text{ h}$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"The IQR covers the middle half of the ports and ignores the tails, so one extreme port cannot distort it. The mean (26.41) above the median (22.40) also shows right skew."}],final:"Report the IQR = 17.25 h → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The range uses the maximum directly, so the single 88.7 h port inflates it most of all.","","The standard deviation squares every deviation, so extreme values pull it up strongly.","SE Mean measures how precisely the mean is estimated (StDev over √N), not the spread of individual ports."],bookRef:"Book pp. 148, 150"},{id:"sv-2-095",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`A team records how many technician visits each of 100 fibre repairs needed:

| Visits needed | Number of repairs |
|---|---|
| 1 | 50 |
| 2 | 30 |
| 3 | 15 |
| 4 | 5 |

What is the mean number of visits per repair?`,options:["25","2.5","1.75","1"],answer:2,hint:"Mean from a frequency table = Σ(value × frequency) over Σfrequency.",steps:[{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\bar{x}=\\dfrac{\\sum x_i f_i}{\\sum f_i}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\bar{x}=\\dfrac{1\\times 50+2\\times 30+3\\times 15+4\\times 5}{50+30+15+5}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\bar{x}=\\dfrac{50+60+45+20}{100}=\\dfrac{175}{100}=1.75$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"On average a repair needs 1.75 visits. Half of all repairs need a repeat visit, which is the target for improvement."}],final:"Mean = 1.75 visits → answer **C**",whyWrong:["25 is the mean of the frequency column (100 divided by 4), not of the visits.","2.5 averages the four visit values (1 to 4) without weighting them by how many repairs had each.","","1 is the mode (the most common value), not the mean."],bookRef:"Book pp. 147–148"},{id:"sv-2-096",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A team summarises 20 ticket resolution times. Someone then adds one more ticket: a **400-hour** outage. Which statistic will change the LEAST?",options:["The mean","The standard deviation","The range","The median"],answer:3,hint:"The median depends only on the middle position, so it resists extreme values; the mean, SD and range do not.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The median is the middle value of the sorted data. Adding one extreme value moves the middle by at most half a position, whatever its size."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The mean, SD and range all use the 400 h value directly, so they jump. This is why the median (with the IQR) is preferred for skewed time data."}],final:"The median is resistant to the outlier → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The mean uses the size of every value, so one 400 h ticket pulls it up sharply.","The SD squares each deviation, so a far-out value inflates it heavily.","The range is the maximum minus the minimum, so the new maximum changes it completely.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 148"},{id:"sv-2-097",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"The time to provision a cloud server is normally distributed with **μ = 40 min** and **σ = 5 min**. What percentage of servers are provisioned in **under 33 min**? Use $\\Phi(1.40)=0.9192$.",options:["8.08%","16.16%","41.92%","91.92%"],answer:0,hint:"z = (x − μ) over σ, then P(X < x) = Φ(z). For a negative z, Φ(−z) = 1 − Φ(z).",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$\\mu=40$, $\\sigma=5$, $x=33$; lower tail."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$z=\\dfrac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$z=\\dfrac{33-40}{5}=\\dfrac{-7}{5}=-1.40$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$P(X<33)=\\Phi(-1.40)=1-0.9192=0.0808$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"About 8.1% of servers are ready in under 33 minutes."}],final:"P = 8.08% → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","16.16% doubles the tail, as if the question asked for both tails (under 33 or over 47).","41.92% reads the area between the mean and z (0.9192 − 0.5) instead of the tail beyond z.","91.92% is Φ(1.40), the area below z = +1.40; the question asks for the area below z = −1.40."],bookRef:"Book pp. 152–153"},{id:"sv-2-098",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"Average speed of answer is normally distributed with **μ = 120 s** and **σ = 15 s**. Management wants the time that **95% of calls** are answered within (the 95th percentile). Use $z_{0.95}=1.645$.",options:["149.4 s","144.7 s","139.2 s","95.3 s"],answer:1,hint:"x = μ + zσ, with z read from the table for the required one-sided area.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$\\mu=120$ s, $\\sigma=15$ s, area to the left 0.95, $z=1.645$."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$z=\\dfrac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; x=\\mu+z\\sigma$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$x=120+1.645\\times 15$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$x=120+24.7=144.7 \\text{ s}$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Ninety-five per cent of calls are answered within about 145 s; 5% wait longer."}],final:"95th percentile = 144.7 s → answer **B**",whyWrong:["149.4 uses z = 1.96, the two-sided 95% value; the 95th percentile is one-sided (z = 1.645).","","139.2 uses z = 1.282, which is the 90th percentile.","95.3 subtracts zσ from the mean, which gives the 5th percentile instead of the 95th."],bookRef:"Book pp. 152–153"},{id:"sv-2-099",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"Measured upload speed on a business fibre plan is normally distributed with **μ = 100 Mbps** and **σ = 4 Mbps**. The contract specification is **92 to 110 Mbps**. What percentage of speed tests fall **outside** the specification? Use $\\Phi(2.00)=0.9772$ and $\\Phi(2.50)=0.9938$.",options:["97.10%","2.90%","2.28%","0.62%"],answer:1,hint:"Out of spec = P(X < LSL) + P(X > USL): convert each limit to z and add the two tail areas.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$\\mu=100$, $\\sigma=4$ Mbps, LSL = 92, USL = 110."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$z=\\dfrac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$z_{LSL}=\\dfrac{92-100}{4}=-2.00 \\qquad z_{USL}=\\dfrac{110-100}{4}=2.50$$"},{title:"Calculate the tails",body:"$$P(X<92)=1-0.9772=0.0228 \\qquad P(X>110)=1-0.9938=0.0062$$"},{title:"Add",body:"$$P(\\text{out})=0.0228+0.0062=0.0290=2.90\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Most of the loss is on the low side because the mean is nearer the LSL (2σ away) than the USL (2.5σ away)."}],final:"2.90% out of specification → answer **B**",whyWrong:["97.10% is the share inside the specification (the yield), not the share outside.","","2.28% counts only the lower tail (below 92) and forgets the upper tail.","0.62% counts only the upper tail (above 110) and forgets the lower tail."],bookRef:"Book p. 153; Book pp. 222–223"},{id:"sv-2-100",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"Jitter on a VoIP link is normally distributed with **μ = 60 ms** and **σ = 8 ms**. What proportion of measurements fall **between 48 and 72 ms**? Use $\\Phi(1.50)=0.9332$.",options:["0.1336","0.4332","0.8664","0.9332"],answer:2,hint:"P(a < X < b) = Φ(z_b) − Φ(z_a). For limits symmetric about μ this is 2Φ(z) − 1.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$\\mu=60$, $\\sigma=8$, limits 48 and 72."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$z=\\dfrac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$z_{48}=\\dfrac{48-60}{8}=-1.50 \\qquad z_{72}=\\dfrac{72-60}{8}=1.50$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$P=\\Phi(1.50)-\\Phi(-1.50)=0.9332-0.0668=0.8664$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"About 87% of measurements lie within ±1.5σ, between the empirical rule’s 68% (±1σ) and 95% (±2σ)."}],final:"P(48 < X < 72) = 0.8664 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["0.1336 is the proportion **outside** 48–72 ms (the two tails together).","0.4332 is the area from the mean to one limit only (0.9332 − 0.5); both halves are needed.","","0.9332 is Φ(1.50), the area below 72 ms; it still includes everything below 48 ms."],bookRef:"Book p. 153"},{id:"sv-2-101",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A team tests 45 repair-visit durations before running a capability study:

\`\`\`
Normality Test: Visit Duration (min)
Anderson-Darling

Mean        47.31
StDev       21.84
N              45
AD          1.284
P-Value    <0.005
\`\`\`

The probability plot bends away from the line at the upper end. At α = 0.05, what is the BEST conclusion and next step?`,options:["Reject normality; find out why the data are non-normal (for example mixed populations, special causes or a natural lower bound) before choosing a capability method","Fail to reject normality, so a normal capability study can go ahead","Accept that the data are normal because the AD statistic is above 1","Delete the longest visits until the AD p-value rises above 0.05"],answer:0,hint:"AD test: H₀ = data are normal. p ≤ α → reject (not normal). Investigate why before choosing a method.",steps:[{title:"Locate and read",body:"P-Value < 0.005 and the plot curves at the upper end, which is a sign of right skew."},{title:"Decide",body:"p < α, so reject H₀: the data do not follow a normal distribution."},{title:"Next step",body:"The book warns against panic or blind transformation: first look for the cause (two kinds of visit mixed together, special causes, or a lower bound at zero). Then use a suitable method, such as a non-normal capability or observed performance."}],final:"Reject normality and investigate the cause → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","p < 0.005 is below α = 0.05, so normality is rejected, not retained.","There is no “AD above 1 means normal” rule, and H₀ is never accepted; the p-value is the decision number here.","Deleting awkward data without a known special cause hides real process behaviour."],bookRef:"Book pp. 155–158; Book p. 221; Book p. 370"},{id:"sv-2-102",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"With a very large sample (for example several thousand data points), the Anderson-Darling test can reject normality even when the departure from normal is too small to matter in practice. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Larger n → more sensitive test. Always look at the probability plot as well as the p-value.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Like any hypothesis test, the AD test gets more powerful as n grows. With very large samples it flags tiny, harmless departures from normality."},{title:"Practice",body:"The book notes that very large samples make the test highly sensitive, so check the probability plot and ask whether the departure matters for the tool being used."}],final:"True: large samples make the AD test very sensitive → answer **True**",whyWrong:["","The test’s power grows with n; with thousands of points even trivial departures give p < 0.05, so the statement is true."],bookRef:"Book p. 157"},{id:"sv-2-103",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Short-call handle times have mean **3.0 min** and standard deviation **2.5 min**. A handle time can never be negative. What does this suggest about the shape of the distribution?",options:["It must be normal because the mean and standard deviation are both known","It is probably right-skewed: the mean is only 1.2 SDs above the zero bound, so a normal curve would wrongly predict about 11.5% negative times","It is probably left-skewed because the values cannot go below zero","It is bimodal because the SD is close to the mean"],answer:1,hint:"z = (0 − μ) over σ shows how far the mean sits above a zero bound. Times bounded at zero with a large SD are usually right-skewed.",steps:[{title:"Calculate the position of zero",body:"$$z=\\dfrac{0-3.0}{2.5}=-1.2 \\qquad \\Phi(-1.2)=0.115$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"A normal model would put about 11.5% of calls below zero, which is impossible. The data must pile up near zero with a long tail to the right."},{title:"Lesson",body:"The book warns that many service and time-based metrics are not normal. Check normality before using tools that assume it."}],final:"Right-skewed because of the zero bound → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Any data set has a mean and SD; knowing them says nothing about shape.","","A floor at zero squeezes the lower tail and leaves a long upper tail, which is right skew. Skew is named after the long tail.","An SD close to the mean suggests skew against a bound, not two peaks; bimodality must be seen in a graph."],bookRef:"Book p. 154; Book pp. 256–260"},{id:"sv-2-104",phase:2,topic:"2.2.4",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"A box plot of 50 handle times shows all of the following EXCEPT:",options:["The median","Whether the data have two separate peaks (bimodal)","The interquartile range","Points beyond 1.5 × IQR from the box, plotted as possible outliers"],answer:1,hint:"Box plot = five-number summary + outliers. It hides the detailed shape, so two peaks can look like one symmetric box.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A box plot summarises the median, quartiles, whiskers and outliers. It does not show how the values are spread **inside** the box."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"The book shows that bimodal data can give a perfectly symmetric box plot; a histogram, dot plot or individual value plot is needed to see two peaks."}],final:"A box plot cannot show bimodality → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The line inside the box marks the median.","","The length of the box runs from Q1 to Q3, which is the IQR.","Points beyond the whisker limits (1.5 × IQR from the box) are shown individually with asterisks."],bookRef:"Book pp. 162–167"},{id:"sv-2-105",phase:2,topic:"2.2.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Minitab output for 15 fault-repair times:

\`\`\`
Stem-and-Leaf Display: Repair Time (h)

Stem-and-leaf of Repair Time  N = 15
Leaf Unit = 1.0

  2    0  58
  6    1  1347
 (4)   2  0256
  5    3  14
  3    4
  3    5  9
  2    6
  2    7  38
\`\`\`

What is the median repair time, and what is the shape of the distribution?`,options:["Median 22 h; left-skewed, because most values are on the low side","Median 4 h; right-skewed","Median 29.1 h; right-skewed","Median 22 h; right-skewed (long tail towards high values)"],answer:3,hint:"Stem-and-leaf: value = stem × 10 + leaf (leaf unit 1). The row marked with brackets holds the median. Skew is named after the long tail.",steps:[{title:"Read the values",body:"Rows give 5, 8 | 11, 13, 14, 17 | 20, 22, 25, 26 | 31, 34 | 59 | 73, 78."},{title:"Find the median",body:"$$\\text{Position}=\\dfrac{n+1}{2}=\\dfrac{15+1}{2}=8 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{8th value}=22 \\text{ h}$$"},{title:"Judge the shape",body:"Most values sit between 5 and 34 h, with a thin tail out to 78 h: right (positive) skew. The mean (29.1 h) is above the median, as expected."}],final:"Median 22 h, right-skewed → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Skew is named after the long tail, not where most values sit. The tail here stretches to 78 h, so it is right skew.","(4) is the count of leaves in the row that holds the median, not the median itself.","29.1 h is the mean of the 15 values; the long tail pulls the mean above the median.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 159–162; Book pp. 256–257"},{id:"sv-2-106",phase:2,topic:"2.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A contact-centre manager wants to compare the **distribution** of handle times for four teams on one graph, seeing each team’s centre, spread and outliers side by side. Which graph is MOST appropriate?",options:["Box plots of handle time by team, drawn side by side","A pie chart of total calls per team","A scatter plot of handle time against team number","A single histogram of all handle times combined"],answer:0,hint:"Comparing a continuous Y across a few groups → side-by-side box plots (or individual value plots).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Box plots compress each group into median, quartiles and outliers, so several groups can be compared at a glance on one axis."},{title:"Caution",body:"The book adds that box plots alone do not prove a difference; a hypothesis test in Analyze does that."}],final:"Side-by-side box plots → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A pie chart shows each team’s share of calls, not the distribution of their handle times.","A scatter plot is for two continuous variables; team is a category.","One combined histogram hides the differences between the teams."],bookRef:"Book pp. 162–164"},{id:"sv-2-107",phase:2,topic:"2.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"A time series plot of daily call volume over 12 weeks shows a **repeating wave**: high on Mondays, falling through the week and lowest on Sundays. What does this pattern MOST likely show?",options:["A cyclical, day-of-week effect: stratify the data by weekday before drawing conclusions","Random common-cause variation that needs no further attention","A measurement-system failure in the call counter","A steady upward trend in volume"],answer:0,hint:"Time series plots reveal patterns over time: trends, shifts and cycles. A repeating cycle → stratify by the time factor.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A time series plot shows data in time order, so patterns tied to time stand out: the book describes waves and dynamic patterns."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A weekly wave means day of week is a likely X. Staffing analyses should treat Mondays and Sundays separately."}],final:"Day-of-week cycle: stratify by weekday → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A regular repeating pattern is not random; it points to a factor tied to time.","A gauge fault would not normally follow the calendar so neatly; the weekly cycle reflects real customer behaviour.","The pattern repeats each week rather than rising steadily."],bookRef:"Book pp. 159, 167–169"},{id:"sv-2-108",phase:2,topic:"2.2.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt runs a graphical summary on only **18** chat wait times. The histogram looks lumpy and uneven, with two bars standing taller than the rest.

\`\`\`
Summary Report for Chat Wait (s)

Anderson-Darling Normality Test
  A-Squared        0.47
  P-Value         0.214
Mean              63.8
StDev             11.2
N                   18
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is BEST?`,options:["The data are proven normal because the p-value is above 0.05","You cannot conclude the data are non-normal; with only 18 points a histogram looks lumpy by chance, so rely on the AD test and probability plot","The data are bimodal, because the histogram has two tall bars","The data are non-normal, because the histogram is not bell-shaped"],answer:1,hint:"Small samples make histograms unreliable for judging shape. AD p > 0.05 → fail to reject normality (not “proven normal”).",steps:[{title:"Locate and read",body:"AD p-value = 0.214 with N = 18."},{title:"Decide",body:"p > 0.05, so you cannot conclude the data are non-normal."},{title:"Why the histogram misleads",body:"The book shows that a sample of about 20 from a truly normal process can look non-normal in a histogram. Shape judgements need more data or the probability plot."}],final:"Cannot conclude non-normal; histogram unreliable at n = 18 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A high p-value means there is not enough evidence of non-normality; it never proves normality.","","With 18 points the bar heights are very sensitive to the bin choice; two tall bars do not show two populations.","The book warns not to judge normality from a small-sample histogram; the AD p-value of 0.214 does not reject normality."],bookRef:"Book pp. 156, 160–161"},{id:"sv-2-109",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"Jitter readings across many VoIP links show an **observed** standard deviation of **2.6 ms**. A measurement study shows the probe itself adds a standard deviation of **1.0 ms**. Assuming independence, what percentage of the **observed variance** is due to the measurement system?",options:["14.8%","17.4%","38.5%","41.7%"],answer:0,hint:"σ²_observed = σ²_process + σ²_measurement. Share of observed variance = σ²_measurement over σ²_observed.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$\\sigma_{obs}=2.6$ ms, $\\sigma_{ms}=1.0$ ms."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\%\\text{ of variance}=\\dfrac{\\sigma_{ms}^2}{\\sigma_{obs}^2}\\times 100$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\%=\\dfrac{1.0^2}{2.6^2}\\times 100=\\dfrac{1.00}{6.76}\\times 100$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\%=14.8\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The true process SD is $\\sqrt{6.76-1.00}=2.4$ ms. On the variance scale, 14.8% would be judged unacceptable against the %Contribution guideline (above 9–10%)."}],final:"Measurement share of variance = 14.8% → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","17.4% divides the measurement variance by the true process variance (1 over 5.76) instead of by the observed variance.","38.5% is the ratio of standard deviations (1.0 over 2.6); a share of variance needs the squared values.","41.7% divides the measurement SD by the true process SD (1.0 over 2.4), mixing both mistakes."],bookRef:"Book pp. 176, 178"},{id:"sv-2-110",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`A packet-delay specification is **48 to 52 ms**. The team must choose the cheapest probe that meets the **10% (ten-bucket) rule** for resolution. Cost falls as resolution gets coarser.

| Probe | Resolution (smallest step it can show) |
|---|---|
| A | 1.0 ms |
| B | 0.5 ms |
| C | 0.2 ms |
| D | 0.01 ms |

Which probe should the team choose?`,options:["Probe A","Probe B","Probe D","Probe C"],answer:3,hint:"Resolution should be no coarser than one-tenth of the tolerance (or of the process spread).",steps:[{title:"Pick the rule",body:"$$\\text{Max resolution}=\\dfrac{USL-LSL}{10}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\text{Max resolution}=\\dfrac{52-48}{10}=\\dfrac{4}{10}=0.4 \\text{ ms}$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"A (1.0) and B (0.5) are too coarse. C (0.2) and D (0.01) pass; C is the coarser, cheaper one."}],final:"Probe C (0.2 ms ≤ 0.4 ms) → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Probe A’s 1.0 ms step is a quarter of the whole tolerance, giving only four “buckets”; it fails the 0.4 ms limit.","Probe B’s 0.5 ms step is coarser than the 0.4 ms limit, so it fails the rule.","Probe D meets the rule but is far finer than needed, so it is not the cheapest probe that meets it.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 179–180; Syllabus 2.3.1"},{id:"sv-2-111",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A finance-reporting team suspects that its monthly **revenue-by-product query** is inaccurate. How should it assess the **accuracy** of this transactional measurement system?",options:["Run the same query three times in a row and check that it returns the same answer","Ask two analysts to run the query and compare their results","Plot the monthly totals on a histogram and check for normality","Run the query for a closed period and reconcile its totals with a known reference, such as the audited financial report for that period"],answer:3,hint:"Accuracy = closeness of the average to a known reference (true value). Precision = closeness of repeated results to each other.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Accuracy is assessed against a **known standard**. In transactional work the book suggests reconciling queries with trusted reference points such as financial reports."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Repeating the query or comparing analysts tests precision. A precise query can still be consistently wrong."}],final:"Reconcile against a known reference → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Re-running checks repeatability (precision); a query can repeat the same wrong answer every time.","Comparing analysts checks reproducibility (precision), not whether the answer is right.","Normality says nothing about whether the figures match the truth.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 181"},{id:"sv-2-112",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which statement BEST describes **repeatability**?",options:["Variation when one appraiser measures the same item several times with the same instrument","Variation between the average results of different appraisers measuring the same items","The difference between the observed average and the reference value","The change in bias over time"],answer:0,hint:"Repeatability = same appraiser, same part, same gauge, repeated (equipment variation). Reproducibility = different appraisers.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book defines repeatability as the variation in measurements from one instrument used several times by one appraiser on the same characteristic of the same part. It is often called equipment variation."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Different appraisers → reproducibility. Offset from the reference → bias. Drift over time → stability."}],final:"Repeatability = one appraiser, repeated measurements → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","That is reproducibility (appraiser-to-appraiser variation).","That is bias, an accuracy property.","That is stability, another accuracy property."],bookRef:"Book pp. 173, 179–180"},{id:"sv-2-113",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"Two quality auditors each score the **same 30 recorded calls** using the same checklist. Auditor 1’s average score is consistently **8 points higher** than Auditor 2’s, although each auditor is very consistent when re-scoring a call. Which component of measurement variation is MOST evident?",options:["Repeatability","Reproducibility","Linearity","Part-to-part variation"],answer:1,hint:"Gap between appraisers on the same items → reproducibility. Inconsistency within one appraiser → repeatability.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Reproducibility is the variation between the averages of different appraisers measuring the same items with the same method and instrument."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Each auditor repeats well, but the two disagree by a consistent 8 points: an appraiser effect. Common fixes are calibration sessions and clearer scoring definitions."}],final:"Reproducibility (appraiser effect) → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Each auditor is consistent on re-scoring, so repeatability looks good.","","Linearity is a change in bias across the measuring range of one gauge, not a gap between appraisers.","Part-to-part variation is real variation between calls, which is what the study wants to see, not an error."],bookRef:"Book pp. 173, 179–180"},{id:"sv-2-114",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A power meter measures a **10.0 dBm** reference signal five times and reads **10.3, 10.1, 10.4, 10.2 and 10.5 dBm**. What is the meter’s **bias**?",options:["+0.30 dBm","+0.16 dBm","−0.30 dBm","10.30 dBm"],answer:0,hint:"Bias = observed average − reference value.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Reference = 10.0 dBm; readings 10.3, 10.1, 10.4, 10.2, 10.5."},{title:"Find the average",body:"$$\\bar{x}=\\dfrac{10.3+10.1+10.4+10.2+10.5}{5}=\\dfrac{51.5}{5}=10.30$$"},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\text{Bias}=\\bar{x}-\\text{Reference}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\text{Bias}=10.30-10.0=+0.30 \\text{ dBm}$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The meter reads 0.3 dBm high on average. Calibration can remove a constant offset like this."}],final:"Bias = +0.30 dBm → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","0.16 is the standard deviation of the five readings (a repeatability measure), not the offset from the reference.","−0.30 subtracts the wrong way round (reference minus average); bias is the observed average minus the reference.","10.30 is the average reading itself; bias is the difference from the reference."],bookRef:"Book pp. 181–182"},{id:"sv-2-115",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A gauge linearity study gives a fitted slope of bias against reference value of **−0.025**. The process variation (6σ) is **6.0 units**. Using the book’s formula, what is the gauge **linearity**?",options:["−0.15","0.0042","0.15","2.5"],answer:2,hint:"Linearity = |slope| × process variation. %Linearity = |slope| × 100.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Slope = −0.025, process variation = 6.0."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\text{Linearity}=|\\text{Slope}|\\times \\text{Process variation}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\text{Linearity}=|-0.025|\\times 6.0$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\text{Linearity}=0.025\\times 6.0=0.15 \\qquad \\%\\text{Linearity}=0.025\\times 100=2.5\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The bias changes across the range by an amount equal to 0.15 units over the process spread. The negative slope means the gauge reads relatively lower at the high end."}],final:"Linearity = 0.15 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["−0.15 keeps the sign of the slope; linearity uses the absolute value of the slope.","0.0042 divides the slope by the process variation instead of multiplying.","","2.5 is %Linearity (the absolute slope × 100), not linearity in measurement units."],bookRef:"Book p. 183"},{id:"sv-2-116",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A latency probe measures a **20.000 ms** reference delay line 30 times:

\`\`\`
Type 1 Gage Study: Latency probe (ms)

Basic Statistics
Reference      20.000
Mean           20.048
StDev           0.031
6 * StDev       0.186
Tolerance       2.000

Bias
Bias            0.048
T                8.48
PValue          0.000
(Test Bias = 0)

Capability
Cg               2.15
Cgk              1.63
\`\`\`

At α = 0.05, which conclusion is correct?`,options:["The probe has no bias, because a bias of 0.048 is tiny compared with the 2.000 tolerance","The probe has a repeatability problem, because the T value is above 2","The probe has a statistically significant bias of about +0.05 ms; correct it by calibration, while its repeatability (StDev 0.031) is small","The probe is unstable, because the mean differs from the reference"],answer:2,hint:"Bias test: H₀ bias = 0. p ≤ α → significant bias → calibrate. Repeatability is read from the StDev of the repeated readings.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Bias = 0.048 ms, PValue = 0.000, StDev = 0.031 ms."},{title:"Decide on bias",body:"p < 0.05 → reject H₀: the probe reads about 0.05 ms high on average."},{title:"Judge precision",body:"The spread of repeated readings (6 × StDev = 0.186) is small against the tolerance (Cg 2.15). Cgk falls to 1.63 because the bias uses up part of the tolerance. Calibration should remove the offset."}],final:"Significant bias; calibrate → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Small relative to the tolerance is a practical judgement; statistically, p = 0.000 shows the bias is real.","T tests the bias against zero; repeatability is judged from the StDev (0.031), which is small.","","Stability is about change over time; a single study can show bias, not drift."],bookRef:"Book pp. 181–183"},{id:"sv-2-117",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"Calibrating a gauge can reduce its bias, but calibration on its own does not improve the gauge’s repeatability. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Calibration fixes location (bias). Repeatability is precision, fixed by maintenance, fixtures, better equipment or averaging repeats.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says calibration can minimise and control bias. Bias is about **where** the readings centre."},{title:"Repeatability",body:"Repeatability is the **scatter** of repeated readings. Shifting the centre does not reduce the scatter, so the statement is true."}],final:"True: calibration fixes bias, not repeatability → answer **True**",whyWrong:["","Calibration adjusts the gauge so its average matches the reference (an accuracy fix); it does not reduce the scatter of repeated readings."],bookRef:"Book pp. 182, 190"},{id:"sv-2-118",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A depot scale is checked with certified weights of **1 kg, 5 kg, 10 kg and 20 kg**. At every weight it reads **0.2 kg heavy**, with very little scatter. Which description fits BEST?",options:["A linearity problem, because the error appears at more than one weight","A stability problem, because the scale is wrong at every weight","A repeatability problem, because the readings are wrong","A constant bias across the range, which can be corrected by calibration (an offset adjustment)"],answer:3,hint:"Same offset at every reference value → constant bias. Offset that grows or shrinks across the range → linearity.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Bias is the offset from the reference. If it is the same everywhere in the range it is a **constant bias**, the book’s scale example."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The +0.2 kg error does not change with the weight, so linearity is fine. A single calibration offset corrects it."}],final:"Constant bias; calibrate → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Linearity means the bias changes across the range; here the bias is the same at every weight.","Stability is a change in bias over time; the stem describes one set of checks, not drift.","Repeatability is scatter in repeated readings, and the stem says the scatter is very small.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 174, 182–183"},{id:"sv-2-119",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"How is the **stability** of a measurement system normally assessed?",options:["By measuring the same reference standard at regular intervals over time and plotting the results on a control chart","By measuring several reference standards across the operating range once and fitting a line","By having three appraisers measure ten parts twice each","By comparing one reading of a reference standard with its certified value"],answer:0,hint:"Stability = bias over time → same reference, measured periodically, on a control chart.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book describes stability as bias characterised as a function of time, tracked with control charts. The first stability study is usually done right after calibration."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Several standards once → linearity. Appraisers × parts × trials → Gage R&R. One reading → bias at a single point."}],final:"Periodic checks of one reference on a control chart → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","That is a linearity study (bias across the range), not stability over time.","That is a crossed Gage R&R study (repeatability and reproducibility).","A single comparison estimates bias at one moment; stability needs repeated checks over time."],bookRef:"Book pp. 173, 183"},{id:"sv-2-120",phase:2,topic:"2.3.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A crossed Gage R&R on a fibre-loss test set gives variance components **Total Gage R&R = 0.0441** and **Part-to-Part = 0.3159**. What is the **%Study Var** for Total Gage R&R?",options:["93.67%","87.75%","35.00%","12.25%"],answer:2,hint:"%Study Var = σ_GRR over σ_total × 100, with σ = √VarComp and VarComp_total = VarComp_GRR + VarComp_part.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$\\sigma^2_{GRR}=0.0441$, $\\sigma^2_{part}=0.3159$."},{title:"Total variance",body:"$$\\sigma^2_{total}=0.0441+0.3159=0.3600$$"},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\%SV=\\dfrac{\\sigma_{GRR}}{\\sigma_{total}}\\times 100=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma^2_{GRR}}{\\sigma^2_{total}}}\\times 100$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$\\%SV=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{0.0441}}{\\sqrt{0.3600}}\\times 100=\\dfrac{0.21}{0.60}\\times 100=35.0\\%$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"35% is above 30%, so the measurement system is unacceptable for studying this process."}],final:"%Study Var = 35.0% → answer **C**",whyWrong:["93.67% is the %Study Var of Part-to-Part, not of Total Gage R&R.","87.75% is the %Contribution of Part-to-Part.","","12.25% is the %Contribution (the variance ratio); %Study Var needs the square root."],bookRef:"Book pp. 185–186"},{id:"sv-2-121",phase:2,topic:"2.3.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A Gage R&R on a cable-length meter gives $\\sigma_{GRR}=0.05$ m. The specification is **LSL = 98.8 m** and **USL = 101.2 m**. Using a study variation of 6 × SD, what is **%Tolerance**?",options:["2.08%","6.25%","12.50%","25.00%"],answer:2,hint:"%Tolerance = (6 × σ_GRR) over (USL − LSL) × 100.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$\\sigma_{GRR}=0.05$ m, $USL-LSL=101.2-98.8=2.4$ m."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\%\\text{Tol}=\\dfrac{6\\,\\sigma_{GRR}}{USL-LSL}\\times 100$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\%\\text{Tol}=\\dfrac{6\\times 0.05}{2.4}\\times 100=\\dfrac{0.30}{2.4}\\times 100$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\%\\text{Tol}=12.5\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Measurement error uses 12.5% of the tolerance: in the 10–30% band, so acceptable for pass/fail use only with caution."}],final:"%Tolerance = 12.5% → answer **C**",whyWrong:["2.08% divides σ_GRR by the tolerance without the 6 × SD study-variation multiplier.","6.25% uses 3 × SD instead of 6 × SD.","","25.00% divides by half the tolerance (1.2 m), which is only correct when there is a single spec limit."],trap:"The book’s cheat sheet uses 5.15 × SD; Minitab’s default is 6 × SD. Use the multiplier the question gives.",bookRef:"Book p. 185"},{id:"sv-2-122",phase:2,topic:"2.3.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"In an Xbar-R Gage R&R, each operator measured every part **twice**. The average range across all parts and operators is $\\bar{R}=0.0564$ mm. Using $d_2=1.128$ for subgroups of 2, what is the repeatability standard deviation $\\sigma_{repeat}$?",options:["0.0636 mm","0.0500 mm","0.0333 mm","0.0282 mm"],answer:1,hint:"σ_repeat = R̄ over d₂, with d₂ chosen for the number of trials per part (2 → 1.128, 3 → 1.693).",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$\\bar{R}=0.0564$ mm, 2 trials, $d_2=1.128$."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\sigma_{repeat}=\\dfrac{\\bar{R}}{d_2}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\sigma_{repeat}=\\dfrac{0.0564}{1.128}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\sigma_{repeat}=0.0500 \\text{ mm}$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"This is the equipment variation. It is combined with reproducibility as variances to get σ_GRR."}],final:"σ_repeat = 0.0500 mm → answer **B**",whyWrong:["0.0636 multiplies R̄ by d₂ instead of dividing.","","0.0333 uses d₂ = 1.693, the constant for three trials; each part was measured twice.","0.0282 divides R̄ by 2 (the number of trials) instead of by d₂."],bookRef:"Book pp. 184–185"},{id:"sv-2-123",phase:2,topic:"2.3.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"A Gage R&R reports $\\sigma_{GRR}=0.40$ and **total** study standard deviation $\\sigma_{total}=1.00$. What is the **number of distinct categories (ndc)**?",options:["2","3","4","7"],answer:1,hint:"σ_part = √(σ²_total − σ²_GRR); ndc = 1.41 × σ_part over σ_GRR, truncated down.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$\\sigma_{GRR}=0.40$, $\\sigma_{total}=1.00$."},{title:"Find σ_part",body:"$$\\sigma_{part}=\\sqrt{\\sigma^2_{total}-\\sigma^2_{GRR}}=\\sqrt{1.00-0.16}=\\sqrt{0.84}=0.9165$$"},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$ndc=1.41\\times \\dfrac{\\sigma_{part}}{\\sigma_{GRR}}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$ndc=1.41\\times \\dfrac{0.9165}{0.40}=1.41\\times 2.291=3.23 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 3$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"ndc = 3 is below 5: the gauge can only sort parts into about three groups, too coarse for studying process variation."}],final:"ndc = 3 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["2 leaves out the 1.41 (√2) factor and truncates σ_part over σ_GRR = 2.29.","","4 rounds 3.23 up; Minitab truncates ndc down to the whole number below.","7 uses the ratio of variances (0.84 over 0.16) instead of standard deviations."],trap:"ndc uses σ_part, not σ_total. Here both truncate to 3, but they will not always.",bookRef:"Book pp. 185–186"},{id:"sv-2-124",phase:2,topic:"2.3.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"A Gage R&R summary shows only the **%Contribution** of Total Gage R&R: **16%**. What is the corresponding **%Study Var**, and what is the verdict under the usual 10% / 30% guideline?",options:["16%, conditionally acceptable","40%, unacceptable","4%, acceptable","40%, conditionally acceptable"],answer:1,hint:"%Study Var = √(%Contribution as a fraction) × 100. Bands: < 10% acceptable, 10–30% conditional, > 30% unacceptable.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"%Contribution (GRR) = 16% = 0.16 of the total variance."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\%SV=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma^2_{GRR}}{\\sigma^2_{total}}}\\times 100$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$\\%SV=\\sqrt{0.16}\\times 100=0.40\\times 100=40\\%$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"40% > 30% → unacceptable. On the variance scale the matching band edge is about 9–10%, which 16% also exceeds."}],final:"%Study Var = 40%, unacceptable → answer **B**",whyWrong:["16% is the %Contribution; the 10/30 guideline applies to %Study Var, which is its square root.","","4% squares the %Contribution (0.16²) instead of taking its square root.","The conversion is right, but 40% is above 30%, which is the unacceptable band."],bookRef:"Book pp. 185–186"},{id:"sv-2-125",phase:2,topic:"2.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`\`\`\`
Gage R&R (ANOVA): Call-duration audit tool (s)

                              %Contribution
Source             VarComp    (of VarComp)
Total Gage R&R      0.3600        14.40
  Repeatability     0.0400         1.60
  Reproducibility   0.3200        12.80
    Operator        0.2900        11.60
    Operator*Part   0.0300         1.20
Part-To-Part        2.1400        85.60
Total Variation     2.5000       100.00
\`\`\`

The measurement system is not acceptable. Where should the team focus FIRST?`,options:["On the tool itself: repair or replace it, because repeatability is the largest error","On the Operator*Part interaction, because it is listed last","On the operators: standardise the measuring method and train them, because reproducibility (mainly the Operator term) is most of the gauge variation","Nowhere: Part-to-Part is 85.6%, so the system is acceptable"],answer:2,hint:"Repeatability large → fix the equipment. Reproducibility (Operator) large → fix the method and training.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Total GRR 14.40% contribution: Repeatability 1.60%, Reproducibility 12.80% (Operator 11.60%, Operator*Part 1.20%)."},{title:"Check the verdict",body:"$$\\%SV_{GRR}=\\sqrt{0.144}\\times 100=37.9\\%$$ This is above 30%, so the system is unacceptable."},{title:"Decide",body:"Almost all of the gauge error comes from differences between operators. The book’s remedies for reproducibility are training and a standard operating procedure for measuring."}],final:"Fix reproducibility: operators and method → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Repeatability is only 1.60% of the variance against 12.80% for reproducibility; the tool repeats well.","The interaction is small (1.20%); the plain Operator effect (11.60%) dominates.","","A large part-to-part share does not make the gauge acceptable. Total GRR %Study Var is √0.144 ≈ 37.9%, which is above 30%."],bookRef:"Book pp. 184–185, 190"},{id:"sv-2-126",phase:2,topic:"2.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A team plans to use a signal-level meter to study **process variation** in Analyze. Its Gage R&R output is:

\`\`\`
Gage R&R: Signal-level meter (dB), tolerance = 10

                              Study Var  %Study Var  %Tolerance
Source            StdDev (SD)  (6 × SD)       (%SV)  (SV/Toler)
Total Gage R&R         0.1420     0.8520      28.40        8.52
  Repeatability        0.1300     0.7800      26.00        7.80
  Reproducibility      0.0571     0.3428      11.43        3.43
Part-To-Part           0.4794     2.8765      95.88       28.76
Total Variation        0.5000     3.0000     100.00       30.00

Number of Distinct Categories = 4
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is BEST for this purpose?`,options:["Use the gauge: %Tolerance is 8.52%, which is below 10%","Use the gauge: Part-to-Part %Study Var is 95.88%, so parts dominate","Reject the gauge outright: %Study Var above 10% is always unacceptable","Improve the gauge first: %Study Var of 28.4% is only marginal and 4 distinct categories is below 5, so it cannot resolve process variation well"],answer:3,hint:"Purpose decides the metric: process improvement → %Study Var and ndc; product conformance → %Tolerance. ndc must be ≥ 5.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Total GRR: %Study Var 28.40, %Tolerance 8.52, ndc 4."},{title:"Apply the rules for this purpose",body:"%Study Var 28.4% is in the 10–30% band (marginal). ndc = 4 < 5, so the gauge can only sort readings into about four groups."},{title:"Decide",body:"Repeatability (26.0%) dominates, so work on the equipment before using the meter to study process variation. The low %Tolerance would matter only for pass/fail checks."}],final:"Marginal %SV and ndc 4 → improve the gauge first → answer **D**",whyWrong:["%Tolerance judges pass/fail decisions against the specification. For studying process variation, %Study Var and ndc are the right measures.","The Part-to-Part figure does not override the GRR verdict; %SV values do not add to 100%, and ndc 4 is still below 5.","The 10–30% band is “conditional” or marginal, not automatically unacceptable; the real problem here is the low ndc combined with a marginal %SV.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 185–186, 190"},{id:"sv-2-127",phase:2,topic:"2.3.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Repeatability is the largest component in a Gage R&R study of a handheld fibre-loss meter. All of the following are reasonable actions to reduce **repeatability** error EXCEPT:",options:["Train all technicians to follow one standard measuring procedure","Service or replace the worn test leads and connectors","Use a fixture that holds the fibre in the same position for every reading","Average several readings for each measurement (signal averaging)"],answer:0,hint:"Repeatability = equipment variation → maintain, fixture, upgrade or average. Reproducibility = appraiser variation → train and standardise the method.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Repeatability is the scatter when the **same** person measures the **same** item repeatedly, so its causes lie in the gauge and set-up."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Standardising the method across technicians reduces differences **between** people, which is reproducibility. The other three act on the equipment or the reading itself."}],final:"Training and standardising targets reproducibility → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Worn connectors make repeated readings scatter, so maintenance targets repeatability.","Fixturing removes positional scatter between repeated readings: a repeatability fix.","The book’s signal averaging reduces repeatability error, at least temporarily."],bookRef:"Book pp. 190, 197"},{id:"sv-2-128",phase:2,topic:"2.3.3",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:"Which sequence is correct for running a crossed Gage R&R study?",options:["Select parts → operators measure the parts in a fixed order, knowing which part is which → calibrate the gauge → analyse the results","Calibrate the gauge → select parts that cover the full process range → operators measure the parts in random order without seeing the labels → repeat the measurements in a new random order → analyse the results","Analyse last year’s data → select parts from the middle of the range → one operator measures each part once → calibrate the gauge","Calibrate the gauge → select parts from within the specification only → operators measure → analyse the results without repeating any measurement"],answer:1,hint:"Calibrate → pick parts across the range → blind, randomised trial 1 → blind, randomised trial 2 (or 3) → analyse.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s steps: calibrate first (bias, linearity, stability), choose parts that represent the whole range of variation, run a **blind** study in **random** order, repeat the trials, and analyse with ANOVA."},{title:"Why the order matters",body:"Calibration first prevents a known offset from contaminating the study; blinding and randomising stop operators remembering earlier readings."}],final:"Calibrate → parts across range → blind random trials → repeat → analyse → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Calibration should come first, and operators should measure blind and in random order so memory does not bias the readings.","","A Gage R&R needs fresh, planned data from several operators measuring each part at least twice; parts must cover the range.","Parts should span the process range, and each part must be measured more than once to estimate repeatability."],bookRef:"Book pp. 192–193"},{id:"sv-2-129",phase:2,topic:"2.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A team wants a Gage R&R on a **cable pull-strength test**. Each test **breaks the sample**, so the same sample cannot be measured twice. Samples are cut in batches from the same drum length, which is assumed to be uniform. Which approach is MOST appropriate?",options:["A crossed Gage R&R, with every operator testing every sample twice","An attribute agreement analysis, because destructive tests give pass/fail results","A nested Gage R&R, with each operator testing different samples taken from the same uniform batch","Skip the MSA, because destructive tests cannot be studied"],answer:2,hint:"Destructive or one-time tests → nested Gage R&R (parts nested within operators, using homogeneous batches).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In a nested design each operator measures **different** parts. It relies on samples from a uniform batch standing in for “the same part”."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The pull test destroys each sample, so a crossed design is impossible. The book names destructive testing as the case for a nested design."}],final:"Use a nested Gage R&R → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A crossed design needs each operator to measure the same part repeatedly, which is impossible when the test destroys the sample.","The test gives a continuous strength value; changing to pass/fail would throw information away.","","Destructive tests can be studied with a nested design; skipping MSA leaves the data unvalidated."],bookRef:"Book p. 191"},{id:"sv-2-130",phase:2,topic:"2.3.4",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"Two appraisers classify 200 trouble tickets as **Network** or **Customer-side**. They agree on **90%** of tickets ($P_o=0.90$). The agreement expected by chance, from their category proportions, is $P_e=0.60$. What is **Cohen’s kappa**?",options:["0.30","0.50","0.75","0.90"],answer:2,hint:"κ = (P_o − P_e) over (1 − P_e). Guideline: κ ≥ 0.7 acceptable, near 0.9 preferred.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$P_o=0.90$, $P_e=0.60$."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\kappa=\\dfrac{P_o-P_e}{1-P_e}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\kappa=\\dfrac{0.90-0.60}{1-0.60}=\\dfrac{0.30}{0.40}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\kappa=0.75$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"κ = 0.75 meets the usual minimum of 0.70, though it is below the preferred 0.90. Agreement is acceptable."}],final:"κ = 0.75 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["0.30 is the raw difference P_o − P_e; it has not been divided by the room for agreement above chance (1 − P_e).","0.50 divides by P_e (0.60) instead of by 1 − P_e (0.40).","","0.90 is the observed agreement P_o; kappa removes the agreement expected by chance."],bookRef:"Book pp. 201–203"},{id:"sv-2-131",phase:2,topic:"2.3.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Three supervisors each classify the same 50 recorded calls as **Pass** or **Fail** (two trials each):

\`\`\`
Attribute Agreement Analysis: Call compliance

Between Appraisers
Assessment Agreement
# Inspected  # Matched  Percent        95% CI
         50         34    68.00  (53.30, 80.48)

Fleiss' Kappa Statistics
Response    Kappa  SE Kappa       Z  P(vs > 0)
Fail       0.5812    0.0816  7.1225     0.0000
Pass       0.5812    0.0816  7.1225     0.0000
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is correct?`,options:["Agreement is acceptable because P(vs > 0) = 0.0000","Agreement is acceptable because 68% matched, which is above 50%","The supervisors are accurate, because they agree with each other most of the time","Agreement between supervisors is not acceptable: kappa 0.58 is below the usual 0.70 minimum"],answer:3,hint:"Kappa: 1 = perfect, 0 = chance only. At least 0.70 is typically required, about 0.90 preferred. P(vs > 0) only tests “better than chance”.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Fleiss’ kappa = 0.5812 for both responses; 34 of 50 calls matched (68%)."},{title:"Decide",body:"κ = 0.58 < 0.70, so the supervisors do not agree well enough. The tiny p-value only rules out chance-level agreement."},{title:"Next step",body:"Clarify the Pass/Fail definitions, build examples of borderline calls and retrain, then repeat the study."}],final:"κ = 0.58 < 0.70 → not acceptable → answer **D**",whyWrong:["That p-value only shows agreement is better than pure chance; it does not show agreement is good enough.","50% is not a criterion; 68% matched with a kappa of 0.58 is weak agreement for a compliance decision.","Between-appraiser agreement says nothing about accuracy; that needs a comparison with a known standard.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 201–203"},{id:"sv-2-132",phase:2,topic:"2.3.4",qtype:"table",difficulty:1,stem:`An appraiser’s Pass/Fail calls on 60 installation photos are compared with an expert standard:

| | Standard: Pass | Standard: Fail |
|---|---|---|
| **Appraiser: Pass** | 40 | 5 |
| **Appraiser: Fail** | 3 | 12 |

What is the appraiser’s **percentage agreement with the standard**?`,options:["86.7%","93.0%","70.6%","13.3%"],answer:0,hint:"Agreement with standard = (cells where appraiser and standard match) over total items × 100.",steps:[{title:"Locate the matching cells",body:"Pass–Pass = 40 and Fail–Fail = 12."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\%\\text{Agree}=\\dfrac{\\text{matches}}{\\text{total}}\\times 100$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\%\\text{Agree}=\\dfrac{40+12}{60}\\times 100=\\dfrac{52}{60}\\times 100=86.7\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The 5 photos the standard failed but the appraiser passed are **missed defects** (5 of 17 Fails, about 29%), the costlier error for the customer."}],final:"Agreement = 86.7% → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","93.0% is agreement on the Pass photos only (40 out of 43); the overall figure must include the Fail photos.","70.6% is agreement on the Fail photos only (12 out of 17).","13.3% is the disagreement rate (8 out of 60), not the agreement rate."],bookRef:"Book pp. 201–202"},{id:"sv-2-133",phase:2,topic:"2.3.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"An attribute agreement study on fibre-splice photos shows low kappa. Most disagreements are on splices with a **small air bubble**: some appraisers pass them and others fail them. What is the BEST improvement before re-running the study?",options:["Agree a clear operational definition and build a set of boundary (limit) sample photos showing what just passes and what just fails","Let each appraiser keep their own judgement, because experience matters more than rules","Increase the number of photos in the study without changing anything else","Switch to a crossed variable Gage R&R on the same photos"],answer:0,hint:"Attribute inspection improves with clear operational definitions, boundary samples, training and good inspection conditions.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book lists ways to improve visual inspection: operational definitions, standards, **boundary samples** and training."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Disagreement is concentrated at the boundary (small bubbles), so defining and showing the boundary is the direct fix. Then repeat the study to confirm."}],final:"Clear definitions and boundary samples → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Personal standards are exactly what causes the disagreement; agreement needs one shared standard.","More photos give a tighter estimate of the same poor agreement; they do not fix it.","The judgement is a pass/fail category; a variable Gage R&R needs a continuous measurement."],bookRef:"Book p. 200"},{id:"sv-2-134",phase:2,topic:"2.3.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In an attribute agreement analysis, a kappa of **0** means:",options:["The appraisers never agree","The appraisers agree no more often than would be expected by chance","The appraisers agree perfectly","The study has too few samples to calculate agreement"],answer:1,hint:"Kappa runs from −1 to +1: 1 = perfect, 0 = chance level, below 0 = worse than chance.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Kappa compares observed agreement with the agreement expected by chance. When the two are equal, kappa is 0."},{title:"Scale",body:"Typically at least 0.70 is required and values near 0.90 are preferred."}],final:"κ = 0 means chance-level agreement → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Never agreeing would give a negative kappa; zero means agreement equal to chance.","","Perfect agreement gives kappa = 1.","Kappa can be calculated; a value of 0 is a result, not a sign of missing data."],bookRef:"Book p. 203"},{id:"sv-2-135",phase:2,topic:"2.4.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"API response time has **LSL = 200 ms** and **USL = 260 ms**. A capability study reports σ(within) = **6 ms** and σ(overall) = **8 ms**. What is **Pp**?",options:["1.25","1.67","2.50","7.50"],answer:0,hint:"Pp = (USL − LSL) over 6σ(overall). Cp uses σ(within).",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$USL-LSL=260-200=60$ ms, $\\sigma_{overall}=8$ ms."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$P_p=\\dfrac{USL-LSL}{6\\,\\sigma_{overall}}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$P_p=\\dfrac{60}{6\\times 8}=\\dfrac{60}{48}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$P_p=1.25$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Long-term potential is 1.25, lower than the short-term Cp of 1.67 because the overall σ includes shifts between subgroups. Neither index checks centring."}],final:"Pp = 1.25 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","1.67 uses σ(within) = 6; that is Cp. Pp uses the overall standard deviation.","2.50 divides by 3σ instead of 6σ.","7.50 divides the tolerance by σ alone, leaving out the 6."],bookRef:"Book p. 216"},{id:"sv-2-136",phase:2,topic:"2.4.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"Router output power has **LSL = 12 dBm** and **USL = 24 dBm**. The process is stable with mean **16.4 dBm** and within-subgroup σ = **1.6 dBm**. What is **Cpk**?",options:["1.58","1.25","0.92","0.46"],answer:2,hint:"Cpk = min[(USL − x̄) over 3σ, (x̄ − LSL) over 3σ], using σ(within).",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"LSL = 12, USL = 24, $\\bar{x}=16.4$, $\\sigma_{within}=1.6$."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$C_{pk}=\\min\\left(\\dfrac{USL-\\bar{x}}{3\\sigma},\\ \\dfrac{\\bar{x}-LSL}{3\\sigma}\\right)$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$CPU=\\dfrac{24-16.4}{3\\times 1.6}=\\dfrac{7.6}{4.8} \\qquad CPL=\\dfrac{16.4-12}{3\\times 1.6}=\\dfrac{4.4}{4.8}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$CPU=1.58 \\qquad CPL=0.92 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; C_{pk}=0.92$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Cp = 1.25 but Cpk = 0.92: the mean sits 1.6 dBm below the midpoint (18). Centring the process would lift Cpk towards 1.25."}],final:"Cpk = 0.92 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["1.58 uses the farther limit (USL); Cpk uses the nearer limit, which gives the smaller value.","1.25 is Cp, which ignores where the mean sits.","","0.46 divides by 6σ instead of 3σ in the Cpk formula."],bookRef:"Book pp. 216, 225"},{id:"sv-2-137",phase:2,topic:"2.4.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"IVR menu-completion time has **LSL = 30 s** and **USL = 60 s**. The mean is **48 s**, σ(within) = **2.5 s** and σ(overall) = **3.2 s**. What is **Ppk**?",options:["0.63","1.25","1.56","1.60"],answer:1,hint:"Ppk = min[(USL − x̄) over 3σ(overall), (x̄ − LSL) over 3σ(overall)].",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"LSL = 30, USL = 60, $\\bar{x}=48$, $\\sigma_{overall}=3.2$."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$P_{pk}=\\min\\left(\\dfrac{USL-\\bar{x}}{3\\sigma_{overall}},\\ \\dfrac{\\bar{x}-LSL}{3\\sigma_{overall}}\\right)$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$PPU=\\dfrac{60-48}{3\\times 3.2}=\\dfrac{12}{9.6} \\qquad PPL=\\dfrac{48-30}{3\\times 3.2}=\\dfrac{18}{9.6}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$PPU=1.25 \\qquad PPL=1.88 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; P_{pk}=1.25$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Long-term, the process falls just short of the 1.33 benchmark on the upper side. Short-term Cpk is 1.60."}],final:"Ppk = 1.25 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["0.63 divides by 6σ instead of 3σ.","","1.56 is Pp, which ignores centring.","1.60 uses σ(within) = 2.5; that is Cpk, not Ppk."],bookRef:"Book pp. 216, 225"},{id:"sv-2-138",phase:2,topic:"2.4.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"A latency process has **LSL = 38 ms**, **USL = 58 ms** and a mean of **50 ms** that cannot be moved. What is the **largest** within-subgroup standard deviation that still gives **Cpk = 1.33**?",options:["10.64 ms","3.01 ms","2.00 ms","1.00 ms"],answer:2,hint:"Rearrange Cpk = (distance to nearer limit) over 3σ → σ = distance over (3 × Cpk).",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Nearer limit: $USL-\\bar{x}=58-50=8$ ms. Target $C_{pk}=1.33$."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$C_{pk}=\\dfrac{USL-\\bar{x}}{3\\sigma} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\sigma=\\dfrac{USL-\\bar{x}}{3\\,C_{pk}}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\sigma=\\dfrac{8}{3\\times 1.33}=\\dfrac{8}{3.99}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\sigma=2.00 \\text{ ms}$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Any σ(within) above about 2 ms leaves Cpk below 1.33. If the mean could be centred (48 ms), a larger σ would be allowed."}],final:"σ_max ≈ 2.00 ms → answer **C**",whyWrong:["10.64 multiplies the distance (8) by 1.33 instead of dividing by 3 × 1.33.","3.01 uses the farther limit (50 − 38 = 12); the nearer limit (USL) controls Cpk.","","1.00 divides by 6 × 1.33; the Cpk formula uses 3σ."],bookRef:"Book pp. 216, 225"},{id:"sv-2-139",phase:2,topic:"2.4.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A capability study of short-term (within) data gives $Z_{USL}=3.6$ and $Z_{LSL}=4.5$. What is **Cpk**?",options:["1.50","1.35","1.20","0.60"],answer:2,hint:"Cpk = Z_min over 3 (short-term Z). Ppk = Z_min over 3 with long-term Z.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$Z_{USL}=3.6$, $Z_{LSL}=4.5$ (within σ)."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$C_{pk}=\\dfrac{Z_{min}}{3}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$C_{pk}=\\dfrac{\\min(3.6,\\ 4.5)}{3}=\\dfrac{3.6}{3}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$C_{pk}=1.20$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The mean sits 3.6σ from the USL and 4.5σ from the LSL. The upper side limits capability."}],final:"Cpk = 1.20 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["1.50 uses the larger Z (4.5); Cpk is set by the side closer to its limit, the smaller Z.","1.35 averages the two Z values before dividing by 3.","","0.60 divides the smaller Z by 6 instead of 3."],bookRef:"Book p. 224"},{id:"sv-2-140",phase:2,topic:"2.4.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"A capability report (overall data) shows **PPM < LSL = 2,500** and **PPM > USL = 4,000**. What is **Z.bench**? Use $\\Phi^{-1}(0.9935)=2.48$, $\\Phi^{-1}(0.9960)=2.65$ and $\\Phi^{-1}(0.9975)=2.81$.",options:["2.48","2.65","2.81","3.98"],answer:0,hint:"Z.bench: add the defect proportions from both tails, then find the z whose upper-tail area equals that total.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"PPM below LSL 2,500; PPM above USL 4,000."},{title:"Combine the tails",body:"$$p_{total}=\\dfrac{2500+4000}{1\\,000\\,000}=0.0065$$"},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$Z_{bench}=\\Phi^{-1}(1-p_{total})$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$Z_{bench}=\\Phi^{-1}(0.9935)=2.48$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Z.bench puts all 6,500 PPM into one tail. It is a long-term (overall) value; adding 1.5 would give a sigma level of about 3.98."}],final:"Z.bench = 2.48 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","2.65 uses only the upper tail (4,000 PPM); Z.bench combines both tails.","2.81 uses only the lower tail (2,500 PPM).","3.98 adds the 1.5 shift; that converts the long-term Z.bench into a short-term sigma level, which the question did not ask for."],bookRef:"Book pp. 217–220, 222–223"},{id:"sv-2-141",phase:2,topic:"2.4.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"Packet loss on a link has an upper limit only: **USL = 6.0%**. Overall data give mean **4.2%** and standard deviation **0.6%**, and the data are normal. How many parts per million (PPM) are expected **above the USL**? Use $\\Phi(3.00)=0.99865$.",options:["66,807","2,700","1,350","0.135"],answer:2,hint:"Z_USL = (USL − x̄) over σ; PPM above USL = [1 − Φ(Z)] × 10⁶.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"USL = 6.0, $\\bar{x}=4.2$, $\\sigma=0.6$; one-sided."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$Z_{USL}=\\dfrac{USL-\\bar{x}}{\\sigma}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$Z_{USL}=\\dfrac{6.0-4.2}{0.6}=\\dfrac{1.8}{0.6}=3.00$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$PPM=(1-0.99865)\\times 10^6=1350$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"About 0.135% of measurements exceed the limit. Ppk = Z over 3 = 1.00, below the 1.33 benchmark."}],final:"PPM above USL = 1,350 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["66,807 is the DPMO of a 3σ process with the 1.5σ shift; here Z = 3 is already long-term, so no shift applies.","2,700 counts both tails (±3σ); there is no lower limit, so only the upper tail is a defect.","","0.135 is the percentage above the USL; PPM needs the proportion × 1,000,000."],bookRef:"Book pp. 222–223"},{id:"sv-2-142",phase:2,topic:"2.4.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`\`\`\`
Process Capability Report for Inlet Temperature (°C)

Process Data           Overall Capability     Potential (Within) Capability
LSL          18.00     Pp       1.42          Cp       1.62
Target           *     PPL      2.01          CPL      2.31
USL          27.00     PPU      0.82          CPU      0.94
Sample Mean  24.40     Ppk      0.82          Cpk      0.94
Sample N       250
StDev(Overall) 1.06
StDev(Within)  0.925
\`\`\`

What is the MAIN reason the process is not capable, and the best first action?`,options:["The spread is too wide; reduce variation before anything else","The process is unstable, because Pp is lower than Cp","The process is off-centre towards the USL; move the mean down towards the midpoint (22.5 °C)","The measurement system is inadequate, because Cpk is below 1"],answer:2,hint:"Cp ≈ Cpk → centred. Cp much greater than Cpk → off-centre (shift the mean). Cp low → too much spread.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Cp 1.62 but Cpk 0.94; CPU 0.94 is the limiting side; the mean 24.40 is above the midpoint 22.5."},{title:"Diagnose",body:"Potential (Cp) is good; actual (Cpk) is poor only on the upper side. The gap is a centring problem."},{title:"Decide",body:"Move the mean towards 22.5 °C. If it were centred, Cpk would rise towards Cp (1.62)."}],final:"Off-centre: shift the mean → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Cp = 1.62 and Pp = 1.42 show the spread would fit the tolerance if the process were centred.","A small Cp–Pp gap (1.62 vs 1.42) is normal; the big gap here is between Cp and Cpk, which is a centring problem.","","Nothing in a capability report measures the gauge; a low Cpk with a high Cp points to centring."],bookRef:"Book pp. 209–210, 216"},{id:"sv-2-143",phase:2,topic:"2.4.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`\`\`\`
Process Capability Report for Packet Latency (ms)

LSL 20.00   USL 40.00   Sample Mean 30.50   Sample N 300
StDev(Within) 2.18      StDev(Overall) 3.68

Potential (Within)        Overall
Cp     1.53               Pp     0.91
Cpk    1.45               Ppk    0.86

Expected Overall Performance:  PPM < LSL 2163.65   PPM > USL 4918.29
\`\`\`

What does the large gap between **Cpk (1.45)** and **Ppk (0.86)** MOST likely indicate?`,options:["The process is off-centre, because Ppk is below Pp","The process is capable, because Cpk is above 1.33","The data must be non-normal, because the two indices disagree","There is large variation between subgroups over time (shifts or drift), so the process is probably not stable"],answer:3,hint:"Within σ = short-term (inside subgroups). Overall σ = long-term (includes shifts between subgroups). Cpk much greater than Ppk → instability over time.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"StDev(Within) 2.18 against StDev(Overall) 3.68; Cpk 1.45 against Ppk 0.86."},{title:"Diagnose",body:"Overall σ is much larger than within σ, so a lot of variation happens **between** subgroups: the mean is shifting or drifting over time."},{title:"Decide",body:"Check a control chart for special causes. The short-term figure is what the process could do; the long-term figure is what the customer gets."}],final:"Between-subgroup variation: likely unstable → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The mean (30.5) is almost at the midpoint (30); Ppk is close to Pp. The issue is overall spread, not centring.","Cpk reflects short-term potential only; the customer experiences the overall performance (Ppk 0.86, about 7,100 PPM).","Within and overall σ differ because of between-subgroup variation, not because of the distribution shape.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 213–216, 225"},{id:"sv-2-144",phase:2,topic:"2.4.1",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`Four vendors supply the same managed-router service. Their capability indices for install time are:

| Vendor | Cp | Cpk | Pp | Ppk |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| A | 1.80 | 1.50 | 1.20 | 0.95 |
| B | 1.40 | 1.35 | 1.35 | 1.30 |
| C | 2.10 | 0.90 | 1.90 | 0.85 |
| D | 1.20 | 1.15 | 1.10 | 1.05 |

Which vendor is expected to deliver the **fewest out-of-specification installs over the long term**?`,options:["Vendor B","Vendor A","Vendor C","Vendor D"],answer:0,hint:"Long-term defect level is predicted by Ppk (overall σ, includes centring). Higher Ppk → fewer defects.",steps:[{title:"Choose the right column",body:"Long-term performance including centring is **Ppk**."},{title:"Compare",body:"Ppk: A 0.95, B 1.30, C 0.85, D 1.05. B is highest."},{title:"Interpret",body:"B is consistent (Cp ≈ Cpk ≈ Pp ≈ Ppk), so what it can do short-term it also delivers long-term."}],final:"Vendor B has the highest Ppk (1.30) → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A has the best Cpk (1.50), a short-term figure; its Ppk of 0.95 shows a poorer long-term result.","C has the best Cp and Pp (spread), but it is badly off-centre: Ppk 0.85 is the worst.","D has the smallest gaps between its indices but a lower Ppk (1.05) than B."],bookRef:"Book pp. 216, 225"},{id:"sv-2-145",phase:2,topic:"2.4.1",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"Ppk is calculated with the within-subgroup standard deviation. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Cp, Cpk → σ(within), short-term. Pp, Ppk → σ(overall), long-term.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The “P” indices (Pp, Ppk) describe **performance** over the long term, so they use the overall standard deviation of all the data."},{title:"Contrast",body:"Cp and Cpk use σ(within), estimated from R̄ over d₂ or the pooled SD, which shows short-term potential."}],final:"False: Ppk uses σ(overall) → answer **False**",whyWrong:["Within-subgroup σ is used for Cp and Cpk; Pp and Ppk use the overall σ.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 216, 225"},{id:"sv-2-146",phase:2,topic:"2.4.1",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:"Following the book’s steps to capability, which sequence is correct after selecting the output to improve?",options:["Collect sample data → calculate Cp/Cpk/Pp/Ppk → check normality → validate specification limits → verify customer requirements","Verify customer requirements → validate specification limits → collect sample data → determine the data type (short- or long-term) → check normality → calculate Z, PPM, yield and Cp/Cpk/Pp/Ppk","Verify customer requirements → collect sample data → calculate Cp/Cpk/Pp/Ppk → validate specification limits → check normality","Validate specification limits → check normality → verify customer requirements → collect sample data → calculate"],answer:1,hint:"Requirements → specs → data → data type (ST/LT) → normality → indices.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book lists seven steps: select the output, verify customer requirements, validate the specification limits, collect sample data, determine the data type (long- or short-term), check for normality, then calculate Z, PPM, yield and the indices."},{title:"Why the order matters",body:"Indices are only meaningful when the limits are real customer requirements and the data are of the right type and (for normal methods) normal."}],final:"Requirements → specs → data → type → normality → indices → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Calculating indices before validating the limits and checking normality can produce meaningless numbers.","","Specification limits must be validated before any index is calculated, and normality must be checked first too.","Normality cannot be checked before the data have been collected."],bookRef:"Book pp. 211–212"},{id:"sv-2-147",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"Before a capability study, a team plots daily average first-response time on an I-MR chart: $\\bar{x}=42$ min and $\\overline{MR}=3.0$ min. Using $E_2=2.66$ (that is, $\\dfrac{3}{d_2}$ with $d_2=1.128$), what is the **upper control limit** of the individuals chart?",options:["44.66 min","49.98 min","51.00 min","51.80 min"],answer:1,hint:"I chart: UCL = x̄ + E₂ × MR̄, with E₂ = 3 over d₂ = 2.66 for moving ranges of 2.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$\\bar{x}=42$, $\\overline{MR}=3.0$, $E_2=2.66$."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$UCL_X=\\bar{x}+E_2\\,\\overline{MR}=\\bar{x}+3\\times\\dfrac{\\overline{MR}}{d_2}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$UCL_X=42+2.66\\times 3.0$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$UCL_X=42+7.98=49.98 \\text{ min}$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Any day above about 50 min is a special-cause signal. It should be investigated before baseline capability is reported."}],final:"UCL = 49.98 min → answer **B**",whyWrong:["44.66 adds E₂ itself (2.66) to the mean and forgets to multiply by the average moving range.","","51.00 uses 3 × MR̄; the moving range must first be converted to σ by dividing by d₂ (that is what E₂ = 2.66 does).","51.80 uses D₄ = 3.267, which is the constant for the moving-range chart’s UCL, not the individuals chart."],bookRef:"Book p. 538; Book p. 211"},{id:"sv-2-148",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A team checks 30 days of dropped-call rate for stability before a capability study:

\`\`\`
Run Chart of Daily Dropped-Call Rate (%)

Number of runs about median:          9    Number of runs up or down:     19
Expected number of runs:           16.0    Expected number of runs:     19.7
Longest run about median:             8    Longest run up or down:         4
Approx P-Value for Clustering:    0.004    Approx P-Value for Trends:  0.384
Approx P-Value for Mixtures:      0.996    Approx P-Value for Oscillation: 0.616
\`\`\`

At α = 0.05, which conclusion is supported?`,options:["There is evidence of a trend, because 19 runs up or down were observed","There is evidence of mixtures, because the mixtures p-value is close to 1","There is evidence of clustering (far fewer runs about the median than expected), which suggests a special cause such as a period-by-period shift","The data are random, because three of the four p-values are above 0.05"],answer:2,hint:"Run chart tests: p < α signals clustering, mixtures, trends or oscillation. Too few runs about the median → clustering; too many → mixtures.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Clustering p = 0.004; mixtures 0.996; trends 0.384; oscillation 0.616."},{title:"Decide",body:"Only clustering is significant (0.004 < 0.05). There are 9 runs against 16 expected, so points bunch together on one side of the median for long stretches."},{title:"Next step",body:"Look for what changed between the clusters (a network event, a configuration change) before treating the process as stable."}],final:"Clustering is significant: possible special cause → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The trend p-value is 0.384, above 0.05; 19 runs up or down is close to the 19.7 expected.","A large p-value gives no evidence of mixtures; only a small p-value (below α) signals one.","","One significant test is enough to signal non-randomness; the clustering p-value is 0.004."],bookRef:"Book pp. 211, 215; Book pp. 523–525"},{id:"sv-2-149",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"All of the following on a control chart are signals of **special-cause** variation EXCEPT:",options:["One point more than 3σ from the centre line","Nine points in a row on the same side of the centre line","Six points in a row steadily increasing","Points scattered randomly about the centre line, all within the control limits"],answer:3,hint:"Common cause = random scatter inside the limits with no pattern. Special cause = a point outside 3σ, runs, trends or other non-random patterns.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Common-cause variation is the random noise of a stable process: points inside the limits with no pattern."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"A point beyond 3σ, 9 in a row on one side and 6 in a row trending are all standard special-cause tests. Random scatter is what stability looks like."}],final:"Random scatter inside the limits is common cause → answer **D**",whyWrong:["This is test 1, the classic special-cause signal.","This is the run test for a shift in the mean (test 2).","This is the trend test (test 3).",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 523–525, 532–536"},{id:"sv-2-150",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"In Measure, a team’s control chart of order-to-activation time shows several special-cause signals. The sponsor says: “Remove all special causes first, and only then measure the baseline.” What is the BEST response?",options:["Record the baseline as the process runs today (for example observed DPMO and Ppk), note that it is unstable, and investigate the special causes as part of the project","Agree, and delay the baseline until the chart has no signals","Report Cpk from the within-subgroup σ only, because it ignores the special causes","Delete the out-of-control points and calculate Cp and Cpk from the rest"],answer:0,hint:"Baseline = how the process performs now (a snapshot). Capability is most meaningful when stable, so state the instability and use long-term or observed measures.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book warns against trying to make the process stable before working on it: the baseline is a snapshot of current performance."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Report long-term or observed performance (DPMO, Ppk) with a clear note that the process is unstable. Finding and removing the special causes becomes part of the project’s work."}],final:"Baseline the current, unstable process honestly → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The book says the baseline is a snapshot of the process as it is; delaying it means the project’s improvement can never be measured against the real starting point.","Cpk shows short-term potential; reporting it alone hides the real long-term performance the customer experiences.","Deleting signals without a known, removed cause flatters the baseline and hides the problem."],bookRef:"Book pp. 215, 217"},{id:"sv-2-151",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A stable contact centre is answering within target on average. A supervisor moves agents between queues **every hour** in reaction to the last hour’s figure, even though the control chart shows no signals. What is the MOST likely effect?",options:["Variation decreases, because each adjustment corrects the last error","Variation increases, because adjusting a stable process in response to common-cause noise (tampering) adds variation","There is no effect, because the process is already stable","The control limits become narrower, showing improvement"],answer:1,hint:"Act only on special-cause signals. Adjusting a stable process for every point (tampering) increases variability.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book notes that adjusting the process on every point increases variability. Only out-of-control signals justify action."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The chart shows no signals, so each hourly change reacts to noise and adds its own variation."}],final:"Tampering increases variation → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Adjusting for random noise pushes the process away from its centre as often as towards it, so spread grows.","","Constant adjustment of a stable process adds variation; it is not neutral.","Over-adjustment widens the spread; narrower limits would need a real reduction in common-cause variation."],bookRef:"Book p. 544"},{id:"sv-2-152",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"An audit of **1,200** activation orders, each with **8** opportunities for error, finds **54** defects. What is the DPMO?",options:["45,000","5,625","4.5%","0.005625"],answer:1,hint:"DPMO = defects over (units × opportunities per unit) × 10⁶.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"D = 54, U = 1,200, O = 8."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$DPMO=\\dfrac{D}{U\\times O}\\times 10^6$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$DPMO=\\dfrac{54}{1200\\times 8}\\times 10^6=\\dfrac{54}{9600}\\times 10^6$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$DPMO=0.005625\\times 10^6=5625$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"About 5,600 DPMO is roughly a 4.0σ process with the 1.5σ shift (6,210 DPMO = 4.0σ)."}],final:"DPMO = 5,625 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["45,000 is DPU × 10⁶ (54 over 1,200); it leaves out the 8 opportunities per order.","","4.5% is DPU (defects per unit) written as a percentage, not DPMO.","0.005625 is DPO (defects per opportunity); it has not been multiplied by 1,000,000."],bookRef:"Book pp. 223–224; Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-2-153",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A stable billing process runs at **6,210 DPMO** (long-term attribute data). Using the 1.5σ shift convention, what is its sigma level? Use $\\Phi^{-1}(0.99379)=2.50$ and $\\Phi^{-1}(0.996895)=2.74$.",options:["1.00","2.50","4.00","4.24"],answer:2,hint:"Z_LT = Φ⁻¹(1 − DPMO over 10⁶); sigma level = Z_LT + 1.5.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"DPMO = 6,210 (long-term)."},{title:"Convert to a yield",body:"$$1-\\dfrac{6210}{10^6}=0.99379$$"},{title:"Find Z long-term",body:"$$Z_{LT}=\\Phi^{-1}(0.99379)=2.50$$"},{title:"Add the shift",body:"$$\\sigma_{level}=Z_{LT}+1.5=2.50+1.5=4.00$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"This matches the standard table: 4.0σ ↔ 6,210 DPMO."}],final:"Sigma level = 4.00 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["1.00 subtracts the 1.5 shift (2.50 − 1.5) instead of adding it.","2.50 is Z long-term; the 1.5 shift has not been added.","","4.24 splits the defects into two tails (3,105 PPM each); attribute DPMO is treated as one tail."],bookRef:"Book pp. 223–224"},{id:"sv-2-154",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"A service process is rated at **3.5σ** (short-term, with the 1.5σ shift convention). What long-term DPMO should the team expect? Use $\\Phi(2.00)=0.97725$ and $\\Phi(3.50)=0.999767$.",options:["977,250","45,500","22,750","233"],answer:2,hint:"Z_LT = sigma level − 1.5; DPMO = [1 − Φ(Z_LT)] × 10⁶.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Sigma level 3.5 (short-term)."},{title:"Remove the shift",body:"$$Z_{LT}=3.5-1.5=2.0$$"},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$DPMO=\\left[1-\\Phi(Z_{LT})\\right]\\times 10^6$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$DPMO=(1-0.97725)\\times 10^6=22\\,750$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"A 3.5σ process produces about 22,750 defects per million opportunities in the long run."}],final:"DPMO = 22,750 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["977,250 is the yield (good opportunities) per million, not the defects.","45,500 counts two tails; the sigma table uses one tail.","","233 forgets to subtract the 1.5 shift (uses Z = 3.5 directly)."],bookRef:"Book p. 223"},{id:"sv-2-155",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A stable p chart of activation failures supports this attribute capability output:

\`\`\`
Binomial Process Capability Analysis: Failed Activations

Summary Stats (using 95.0% confidence)
Total samples      12000
Total defectives     282
%Defective:         2.35
Lower CI:           2.09
Upper CI:           2.64
PPM Def:           23500
Process Z:         1.9863
Lower CI:          1.9370
Upper CI:          2.0362
\`\`\`

Minitab’s Process Z here is long-term. Using the 1.5σ shift convention, what is the process **sigma level**?`,options:["1.99","0.49","3.49","3.85"],answer:2,hint:"Attribute capability gives a long-term Z; sigma level = Z_LT + 1.5.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Process Z = 1.9863 (long-term, from 2.35% defective)."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\sigma_{level}=Z_{LT}+1.5$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\sigma_{level}=1.99+1.5=3.49$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"About 3.5σ. The capability is valid only because the p chart shows the process is stable."}],final:"Sigma level ≈ 3.49 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["1.99 is the Process Z (long-term) itself; the 1.5 shift has not been added.","0.49 subtracts the 1.5 shift instead of adding it.","","3.85 adds 1.5 to the %Defective (2.35) instead of to the Z value."],bookRef:"Book pp. 221–225"},{id:"sv-2-156",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"table",difficulty:3,stem:`A provisioning process has two inspection points, each checking the same **500** orders:

| Step | Orders | Opportunities per order | Defects found |
|---|---|---|---|
| Order entry | 500 | 4 | 30 |
| Network configuration | 500 | 6 | 18 |

What is the overall DPMO for the process?`,options:["96,000","24,000","10,500","9,600"],answer:3,hint:"Overall DPMO = total defects over total opportunities × 10⁶ (pool, do not average).",steps:[{title:"Total defects",body:"$$D=30+18=48$$"},{title:"Total opportunities",body:"$$TOP=500\\times 4+500\\times 6=2000+3000=5000$$"},{title:"Pick the formula and calculate",body:"$$DPMO=\\dfrac{D}{TOP}\\times 10^6=\\dfrac{48}{5000}\\times 10^6=9600$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The step DPMOs are 15,000 (entry) and 6,000 (configuration). Pooling weights each step by its opportunities, so the overall figure is 9,600, not their simple average."}],final:"Overall DPMO = 9,600 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["96,000 uses DPU (48 defects over 500 orders) × 10⁶ and ignores opportunities.","24,000 uses only the order-entry opportunities (500 × 4) for all 48 defects.","10,500 averages the two step DPMOs (15,000 and 6,000) instead of pooling defects and opportunities.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 223–224"},{id:"sv-2-157",phase:2,topic:"2.4.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`During Analyze, the team keeps an I chart of the baseline first-time-fix rate. A vendor changed its field staffing just before day 23.

\`\`\`
Test Results for I Chart of First-Time-Fix Rate (%)

TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  23

TEST 2. 9 points in a row on same side of center line.
Test Failed at points:  31, 32, 33
\`\`\`

What is the MOST appropriate action?`,options:["Recalculate the control limits at once, using all 33 points","Ignore the signals because first-time fix is still above the customer’s minimum","Delete days 23–33 and report the baseline from days 1–22 only","Investigate the special cause around day 23 (for example the vendor change) and confirm whether the shift is real and lasting before revising the baseline"],answer:3,hint:"Signal → investigate the special cause first. Recalculate limits only after a verified, sustained shift. Never judge a control chart against specs.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Test 1 at point 23 (a sudden change) and Test 2 at points 31–33 (a sustained shift from point 23 onward)."},{title:"Decide",body:"Both signals line up with the vendor change. Find out what happened; if a real, lasting shift is confirmed, the baseline and centre line are revised from the shift point."},{title:"Book rule",body:"The book recalculates the centre line and limits only after a verified shift, preferably confirmed with a hypothesis test."}],final:"Investigate the special cause before revising anything → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Including the shifted points inflates the limits and hides the signal; limits are revised only after a verified, lasting change.","Specification limits are not control limits; the chart shows the process has changed, which matters for the baseline whatever the spec.","Deleting data without understanding the cause hides what is happening to the process.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 525, 553"},{id:"sv-2-158",phase:2,topic:"2.4.4",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"When a control chart is set up to monitor a process, its control limits should be set from the customer’s specification limits. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Control limits = voice of the process (calculated from data). Specification limits = voice of the customer. Keep them apart.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Control limits are calculated from the process’s own variation (centre line ± 3σ). They show what the process does, not what the customer wants."},{title:"Why it matters",body:"The book warns never to put specs on a control chart: a process can be in control and still not capable, or capable and out of control."}],final:"False: control limits come from process data → answer **False**",whyWrong:["Control limits come from the process data (±3σ, the voice of the process); specification limits are the voice of the customer and are never drawn as control limits.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 525; Book p. 518"},{id:"sv-2-159",phase:2,topic:"2.4.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"To monitor the baseline repair time, a team samples **five jobs every Tuesday morning from the day shift only**. The night shift and the outsourced vendor handle 40% of the jobs. What is the MAIN problem with this monitoring plan?",options:["The sample is biased: it misses the night shift and the vendor, so the chart cannot represent the whole process","Five jobs per subgroup is too many for a control chart","Weekly sampling is always too frequent","Repair time should be monitored with an attribute chart instead"],answer:0,hint:"Monitoring data must represent the whole process: sample across shifts, vendors and days so the chart is not biased.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book stresses that sampling must not bias the chart, for example by taking data from only one shift or one vendor."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Forty per cent of the work, and possibly most of the variation, is never sampled. Spread the samples across shifts, days and the vendor."}],final:"Biased sampling: one shift, no vendor → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Subgroups of 3–5 are standard for an Xbar-R chart; the size is fine.","Frequency should match how fast the process changes; the real flaw here is which jobs are sampled.","Repair time is continuous, so a variables chart is right."],bookRef:"Book p. 517; Book pp. 528–529"},{id:"sv-2-160",phase:2,topic:"2.4.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Following Y = f(X), what is the usual recommendation for monitoring and controlling a process?",options:["Monitor only the inputs; the output takes care of itself","Monitor the output Y to see performance, and control the vital few X’s that drive it","Monitor only the output Y, because inputs do not need control","Monitor every input and output equally to be safe"],answer:1,hint:"Monitor the Y (statistical process monitoring); control the vital few X’s (SPC on inputs).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s rule is: monitor the Y, control the X’s. Charts on the output show performance, while charts or controls on the key inputs prevent problems at the source."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Watching only X or only Y leaves a gap, and charting everything hides the vital few."}],final:"Monitor Y, control the vital X’s → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Without watching Y, the team cannot see whether the X controls actually deliver the result the customer needs.","","Watching only Y means reacting after defects occur; controlling the X’s prevents them.","Charting everything wastes effort and buries the important signals; focus on the vital few X’s."],bookRef:"Book pp. 516, 526, 530"}];export{e as items};
